QZ.load("phys", {"chapters":{"1":["1","Making measurements"],"2":["2","Describing motion"],"3":["3","Forces and motion"],"4":["4","Turning effects of forces"],"5":["5","Forces and matter"],"6":["6","Energy stores and transfers"],"7":["7","Energy resources"],"8":["8","Work and power"],"9":["9","The kinetic particle model of matter"],"10":["10","Thermal properties of matter"],"11":["11","Thermal energy transfers"],"12":["12","Sound"],"13":["13","Light"],"14":["14","Properties of waves"],"15":["15","The electromagnetic spectrum"],"16":["16","Magnetism"],"17":["17","Static electricity"],"18":["18","Electrical quantities"],"19":["19","Electric circuits and electrical safety"],"20":["20","Electromagnetic forces"],"21":["21","Electromagnetic induction"],"22":["22","The nuclear atom"],"23":["23","Radioactivity"],"24":["24","Earth and the Solar System"],"25":["25","Stars and the Universe"]},"topics":{"phys-form":["Formulas","Write the formula"],"phys-unit":["Units & values","Recall"],"phys-law":["Laws & rules","State it"],"phys-expl":["Describe & explain","Describe or explain"],"phys-diff":["Differences","State the differences"],"phys-exp":["Experiments","Describe the experiment"],"phys-facts":["Key points","Answer"]},"cards":[["n-p1-exp-measuring-length-and-volum","phys-expl",1,"Measuring length and volume","<p><strong>Ruler:</strong> read at eye level (to avoid parallax) and measure from the zero mark, checking for a zero error. <strong>Measuring cylinder:</strong> stand it on a flat surface and read the <strong>bottom of the meniscus</strong> at eye level. Read every scale to the <strong>nearest half of its smallest division</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-exp-measuring-time","phys-expl",1,"Measuring time","<p>Use a <strong>stop-watch or clock</strong> for longer intervals; human reaction time (about 0.2 s) adds an error at the start and end. Use a <strong>digital timer with light gates</strong> for short intervals, because it starts and stops automatically.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-exp-measuring-multiples","phys-expl",1,"Measuring multiples","<p>To find a <strong>small distance</strong> or a <strong>short time</strong>, measure many and divide. e.g. thickness of one sheet = thickness of 100 sheets ÷ 100; <strong>period of a pendulum</strong> = time for 20 oscillations ÷ 20. One oscillation is from one side to the other <strong>and back</strong>. This reduces the effect of reading and reaction-time errors.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-for-density","phys-form",1,"Density","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">ρ = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">m</span><span class=\"de\">V</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>ρ</b> = density (kg/m³ or g/cm³)</li><li><b>m</b> = mass (kg or g)</li><li><b>V</b> = volume (m³ or cm³)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>m = ρV</b> · <b>V = m ÷ ρ</b></p>",0,""],["n-p1-uni-length-area-and-volume-con","phys-unit",1,"Length, area and volume conversions","<p>1 km = 1000 m; 1 m = 100 cm = 1000 mm; 1 dm = 0.1 m; 1 cm = 0.01 m; 1 mm = 0.001 m; 1 µm = 0.000 001 m (10⁻⁶ m). 1 m² = 10 000 cm². <strong>1 cm³ = 0.000 001 m³ (10⁻⁶ m³)</strong>; <strong>1 dm³ = 0.001 m³ = 1 litre</strong>; <strong>1 cm³ = 1 ml</strong>. Density: <strong>1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-uni-prefixes-syllabus-section","phys-unit",1,"Prefixes (syllabus Section 4)","<p><strong>M</strong> mega = 10⁶; <strong>k</strong> kilo = 10³; <strong>c</strong> centi = 10⁻²; <strong>m</strong> milli = 10⁻³. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Also <strong>G</strong> giga = 10⁹; <strong>µ</strong> micro = 10⁻⁶; <strong>n</strong> nano = 10⁻⁹.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-exp-density-of-a-regularly-sha","phys-exp",1,"Density of a regularly shaped solid","<p>Measure the length, width and height with a ruler; V = l × w × h. Find the mass on a balance. ρ = m ÷ V.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-exp-density-of-an-irregularly","phys-exp",1,"Density of an irregularly shaped solid that sinks","<p><strong>Displacement:</strong> half-fill a measuring cylinder with water and read the volume; lower the object in on a thread; read the new volume. <strong>Volume of object = rise in water level.</strong> (Or use a displacement (Eureka) can and collect the overflow in a measuring cylinder.) Mass on a balance; ρ = m ÷ V.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-exp-density-of-a-liquid","phys-exp",1,"Density of a liquid","<p>Find the mass of an empty measuring cylinder; pour in liquid and read its volume; find the mass again. Mass of liquid = difference. ρ = m ÷ V.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-fac-an-object-floats-in-a-liqu","phys-facts",1,"When does an object float or sink in a liquid?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">An object <strong>floats</strong> in a liquid if its density is <strong>less</strong> than the liquid's density; it <strong>sinks</strong> if its density is greater.</p>",0,""],["n-p1-fac-one-liquid-floats-on-anoth","phys-facts",1,"When does one liquid float on another?","<p class=\"qn-lead\"><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> One liquid floats on another liquid with a <strong>higher</strong> density, if the two liquids do not mix.</p>",1,""],["n-p2-for-speed","phys-form",2,"Speed","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">v = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">s</span><span class=\"de\">t</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>v</b> = speed (m/s)</li><li><b>s</b> = distance (m)</li><li><b>t</b> = time (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>s = vt</b> · <b>t = s ÷ v</b></p>",0,""],["n-p2-for-average-speed","phys-form",2,"Average speed","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">average speed = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">total distance travelled</span><span class=\"de\">total time taken</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Use total distance ÷ total time, not the average of the speeds.</p>",0,""],["n-p2-for-acceleration","phys-form",2,"Acceleration","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">a = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Δv</span><span class=\"de\">Δt</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>a</b> = acceleration (m/s²)</li><li><b>Δv</b> = change in velocity (m/s)</li><li><b>Δt</b> = time taken (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>Δv = aΔt</b> · <b>Δt = Δv ÷ a</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">Δv = final velocity − initial velocity; it is negative for a deceleration.</p>",1,""],["n-p2-uni-speed-conversions","phys-unit",2,"Speed conversions","<p>km/h → m/s: <strong>÷ 3.6</strong>. m/s → km/h: <strong>× 3.6</strong>. e.g. 72 km/h = 20 m/s.</p>",0,""],["n-p2-dif-distance-time-graph-vs-spe","phys-diff",2,"Distance–time graph vs speed–time graph","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Distance–time graph</th><th>Speed–time graph</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Horizontal line</td><td><strong>At rest</strong></td><td><strong>Constant speed</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Straight sloping line</td><td>Constant speed</td><td><strong>Constant acceleration</strong> (or deceleration if sloping down)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Curve getting steeper</td><td>Accelerating</td><td><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Increasing acceleration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Curve getting less steep</td><td>Decelerating</td><td><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Decreasing acceleration</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gradient gives</td><td><strong>Speed</strong></td><td><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Acceleration</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Area under the line gives</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Distance travelled</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p2-fac-speed-gradient-of-a-distan","phys-facts",2,"What does the gradient of a distance–time graph give?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Speed = gradient of a distance–time graph (straight-line part).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Gradient = rise ÷ run, using a large triangle.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p2-fac-distance-travelled-area-un","phys-facts",2,"What does the area under a speed–time graph give?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Distance travelled = area under a speed–time graph.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Split the area into rectangles and triangles (area of triangle = ½ × base × height).</p></div>",0,""],["n-p2-fac-acceleration-gradient-of-a","phys-facts",2,"What does the gradient of a speed–time graph give?","<p class=\"qn-lead\"><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Acceleration = gradient of a speed–time graph.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>A negative gradient means deceleration.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p2-fac-the-acceleration-of-free-f","phys-facts",2,"Acceleration of free fall near the Earth&#x27;s surface?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The acceleration of free fall g for an object near the surface of the Earth is approximately constant and is approximately 9.8 m/s².</p>",0,""],["n-p3-exp-terminal-velocity","phys-expl",3,"Terminal velocity","<p>A falling object speeds up; drag (air resistance) increases with speed until <strong>drag = weight</strong>. The resultant force is then zero, so the object falls at a <strong>constant, maximum speed: terminal velocity</strong>. <strong>Without air resistance</strong>, every object falls with constant acceleration g. Opening a parachute increases drag, so the object decelerates to a new, lower terminal velocity.</p>",1,""],["n-p3-exp-motion-in-a-circle","phys-expl",3,"Motion in a circle","<p>An object moving in a circle needs a force <strong>perpendicular to its motion, towards the centre</strong>. Its speed stays the same but its direction (so velocity) changes. (a) For the same mass and radius, a <strong>larger force means a higher speed</strong>. (b) For the same mass and speed, a <strong>larger force means a smaller radius</strong>. (c) A <strong>larger mass needs a larger force</strong> for the same speed and radius. (F = mv²/r is not required.)</p>",1,""],["n-p3-for-weight","phys-form",3,"Weight","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">W = mg</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>W</b> = weight (N)</li><li><b>m</b> = mass (kg)</li><li><b>g</b> = gravitational field strength (N/kg)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>m = W ÷ g</b> · <b>g = W ÷ m</b></p>",0,""],["n-p3-for-force-mass-and-acceleratio","phys-form",3,"Force, mass and acceleration","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">F = ma</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>F</b> = resultant force (N)</li><li><b>m</b> = mass (kg)</li><li><b>a</b> = acceleration (m/s²)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>a = F ÷ m</b> · <b>m = F ÷ a</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">The force and the acceleration are in the same direction.</p>",1,""],["n-p3-for-momentum","phys-form",3,"Momentum","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">p = mv</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>p</b> = momentum (kg m/s)</li><li><b>m</b> = mass (kg)</li><li><b>v</b> = velocity (m/s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>v = p ÷ m</b></p>",1,""],["n-p3-for-impulse","phys-form",3,"Impulse","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">impulse = FΔt = Δ(mv)</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>F</b> = force (N)</li><li><b>Δt</b> = time the force acts (s)</li><li><b>Δ(mv)</b> = change in momentum (kg m/s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Unit N s. Change in momentum = mv − mu (final − initial).</p>",1,""],["n-p3-for-resultant-force-from-momen","phys-form",3,"Resultant force from momentum","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">F = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Δp</span><span class=\"de\">Δt</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>F</b> = resultant force (N)</li><li><b>Δp</b> = change in momentum (kg m/s)</li><li><b>Δt</b> = time (s)</li></ul>",1,""],["n-p3-uni-acceleration-of-free-fall","phys-unit",3,"Acceleration of free fall","<p><strong>g = 9.8 m/s²</strong> (= 9.8 N/kg) near the Earth's surface.</p>",0,""],["n-p3-law-newton-s-first-law","phys-law",3,"Newton's first law","<p><strong>An object either remains at rest or continues in a straight line at constant speed unless acted on by a resultant force.</strong> A resultant force may change the velocity of an object by changing its <strong>speed</strong> or its <strong>direction</strong> of motion.</p>",0,""],["n-p3-law-conservation-of-momentum","phys-law",3,"Conservation of momentum","<p><strong>When objects collide or explode, the total momentum before equals the total momentum after</strong>, provided no external force acts. Take one direction as positive; velocities in the opposite direction are negative.</p>",1,""],["n-p3-dif-mass-vs-weight","phys-diff",3,"Mass vs weight","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Mass</th><th>Weight</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What it is</td><td>Quantity of matter</td><td>Gravitational force on a mass</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Unit</td><td>kg</td><td>N</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Changes with location?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong> (depends on g)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Measured with</td><td>Balance</td><td>Force meter (newton meter)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scalar or vector</td><td>Scalar</td><td>Vector</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p3-fac-weights-and-masses-can-be","phys-facts",3,"How can weights (and masses) be compared?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Weights (and masses) can be compared using a balance.</p>",0,""],["n-p3-fac-forces-can-change-the-size","phys-facts",3,"What can forces change about an object?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Forces can change the size, shape, speed and direction of motion of an object.</p>",0,""],["n-p3-fac-find-the-resultant-of-two","phys-facts",3,"How do you find the resultant of two vectors at right angles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\"><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Find the resultant of two vectors at right angles by scale drawing or Pythagoras.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Limited to forces or velocities.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p4-exp-stability","phys-expl",4,"Stability","<p>An object is <strong>more stable</strong> if it has a <strong>low centre of gravity</strong> and a <strong>wide base</strong>. It <strong>topples</strong> when its centre of gravity moves outside its base, because its weight then has a moment that turns it further over.</p>",0,""],["n-p4-for-moment-of-a-force","phys-form",4,"Moment of a force","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">moment = F × d</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>F</b> = force (N)</li><li><b>d</b> = perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of the force (m)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Unit N m. In equilibrium: sum of clockwise moments = sum of anticlockwise moments.</p>",0,""],["n-p4-law-principle-of-moments","phys-law",4,"Principle of moments","<p><strong>For an object in equilibrium, the sum of the clockwise moments about any point equals the sum of the anticlockwise moments about that point.</strong> <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Use it with more than one force on each side of the pivot: add all the clockwise moments and all the anticlockwise moments.</p>",1,""],["n-p4-exp-centre-of-gravity-of-an-ir","phys-exp",4,"Centre of gravity of an irregularly shaped plane lamina","<p>Make three small holes near the edge. Hang the lamina from a pin through one hole so it swings freely; hang a <strong>plumb line</strong> from the same pin. When still, draw a line along the plumb line. Repeat from another hole. The centre of gravity is where the lines <strong>cross</strong>; the third hole is a check.</p>",0,""],["n-p4-exp-no-resultant-moment-in-equ","phys-exp",4,"No resultant moment in equilibrium","<p>Balance a metre rule on a pivot at its centre. Hang different weights at different distances on both sides until it balances again. Calculate clockwise and anticlockwise moments: they are <strong>equal</strong>. The forces up (pivot) and down (weights) are also equal, so there is no resultant force.</p>",1,""],["n-p4-fac-turning-effect-increases-i","phys-facts",4,"Two ways to increase a turning effect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Turning effect increases if the force is bigger or acts further from the pivot.</p>",0,""],["n-p4-fac-the-pivot-force-is-equal-t","phys-facts",4,"Pivot force when a beam is balanced?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The pivot force is equal to the total downward force when a beam is balanced.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>No resultant force.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p5-exp-pressure-in-everyday-examp","phys-expl",5,"Pressure in everyday examples","<p><strong>Large area → small pressure:</strong> wide tyres, snowshoes, tractor tyres, foundations. <strong>Small area → large pressure:</strong> a sharp knife, a drawing pin, stiletto heels. A bigger force on the same area also gives a bigger pressure.</p>",0,""],["n-p5-exp-pressure-in-a-liquid","phys-expl",5,"Pressure in a liquid","<p>Pressure beneath the surface of a liquid <strong>increases with depth</strong> and with the <strong>density</strong> of the liquid, and acts <strong>in all directions</strong>. This is why dams are thicker at the bottom.</p>",0,""],["n-p5-for-pressure","phys-form",5,"Pressure","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">p = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">F</span><span class=\"de\">A</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>p</b> = pressure (Pa)</li><li><b>F</b> = force (N)</li><li><b>A</b> = area (m²)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>F = pA</b> · <b>A = F ÷ p</b></p>",0,""],["n-p5-for-spring-constant","phys-form",5,"Spring constant","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">k = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">F</span><span class=\"de\">x</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>k</b> = spring constant (N/m)</li><li><b>F</b> = force or load (N)</li><li><b>x</b> = extension (m)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>F = kx</b> · <b>x = F ÷ k</b></p>",1,""],["n-p5-for-change-in-pressure-in-a-li","phys-form",5,"Change in pressure in a liquid","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">Δp = ρgΔh</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>Δp</b> = change in pressure (Pa)</li><li><b>ρ</b> = density of the liquid (kg/m³)</li><li><b>g</b> = 9.8 N/kg</li><li><b>Δh</b> = change in depth (m)</li></ul>",1,""],["n-p5-for-stretched-length","phys-form",5,"Stretched length","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">stretched length = original length + extension</div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>extension = stretched length − original length</b></p>",0,""],["n-p5-uni-pressure-units","phys-unit",5,"Pressure units","<p>1 Pa = 1 N/m². 1 N/cm² = 10 000 Pa. 1 kPa = 1000 Pa.</p>",0,""],["n-p5-law-hooke-s-law","phys-law",5,"Hooke's law","<p><strong>The extension of a spring is directly proportional to the load (force) applied, provided the limit of proportionality is not exceeded.</strong> F = kx.</p>",0,""],["n-p5-exp-load-extension-graph-for-a","phys-exp",5,"Load–extension graph for a spring","<p>Clamp the spring next to a vertical metre rule; record the original length (use a pointer, read at eye level). Add 100 g masses one at a time (each 1.0 N); record the length each time. Extension = length − original length. Plot <strong>load (y) against extension (x)</strong> or extension against load; the straight part shows proportionality. Remove the masses to check the spring returns to its original length.</p>",0,""],["n-p5-fac-forces-can-change-the-size","phys-facts",5,"Apart from motion, what can a force change about an object?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Forces can change the size and shape of an object.</p>",0,""],["n-p5-fac-pressure-in-a-liquid-does","phys-facts",5,"What does pressure in a liquid depend on, and not depend on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pressure in a liquid does not depend on the shape of the container, only on depth and density.</p>",0,""],["n-p6-exp-energy-transfers","phys-expl",6,"Energy transfers","<p>Energy is transferred between stores by <strong>forces</strong> (mechanical work done), <strong>electrical currents</strong> (electrical work done), <strong>heating</strong>, and by <strong>electromagnetic, sound and other waves</strong>. e.g. a falling ball: gravitational potential → kinetic (by the force of gravity); a kettle: electrical work → internal energy of the water (by heating).</p>",0,""],["n-p6-for-conservation-of-energy-in","phys-form",6,"Conservation of energy in a transfer","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">total energy input = useful energy output + wasted energy</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. a cyclist going uphill: chemical energy in = kinetic + gravitational potential + thermal energy out.</p>",0,""],["n-p6-for-efficiency-energy","phys-form",6,"Efficiency (energy)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">efficiency = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">useful energy output</span><span class=\"de\">total energy input</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">No unit. Always less than 1 (less than 100%).</p>",1,""],["n-p6-for-percentage-efficiency","phys-form",6,"Percentage efficiency","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% efficiency = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">useful energy output</span><span class=\"de\">total energy input</span></span> × 100%</div>",1,""],["n-p6-for-change-in-gravitational-po","phys-form",6,"Change in gravitational potential energy","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">ΔEₚ = mgΔh</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>ΔEₚ</b> = change in g.p.e. (J)</li><li><b>m</b> = mass (kg)</li><li><b>g</b> = 9.8 N/kg</li><li><b>Δh</b> = change in height (m)</li></ul>",1,""],["n-p6-for-kinetic-energy","phys-form",6,"Kinetic energy","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">Eₖ = ½mv²</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>Eₖ</b> = kinetic energy (J)</li><li><b>m</b> = mass (kg)</li><li><b>v</b> = speed (m/s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>v = √(2Eₖ ÷ m)</b></p>",1,""],["n-p6-law-principle-of-conservation","phys-law",6,"Principle of conservation of energy","<p><strong>Energy cannot be created or destroyed; it can only be transferred from one store to another.</strong> The total energy stays the same. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Apply it to multi-stage examples, e.g. g.p.e. at the top of a slide = k.e. at the bottom + energy wasted heating the slide.</p>",1,""],["n-p6-law-what-g-p-e-and-k-e-depend","phys-law",6,"What g.p.e. and k.e. depend on","<p><strong>G.p.e. depends on weight (mg) and height:</strong> doubling either doubles the g.p.e. <strong>K.e. depends on mass and speed squared:</strong> doubling the speed makes k.e. <strong>4 times</strong> bigger; tripling it makes k.e. <strong>9 times</strong> bigger.</p>",0,""],["n-p6-fac-friction-is-reduced-by-lub","phys-facts",6,"How is friction reduced?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Friction is reduced by lubrication.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Less energy is wasted heating moving parts.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p6-fac-wasted-energy-is-mostly-tr","phys-facts",6,"Where does wasted energy mostly go?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Wasted energy is mostly transferred to the surroundings as thermal energy (heat) and sound.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It spreads out and cannot easily be used again.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p6-fac-a-percentage-efficiency-gr","phys-facts",6,"Can a percentage efficiency be greater than 100%?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A percentage efficiency greater than 100% is impossible.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>It would mean energy is being created.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p6-fac-when-an-object-falls-witho","phys-facts",6,"Falling object (no air resistance): how are g.p.e. and k.e. linked?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">When an object falls (without air resistance), the g.p.e. lost = the k.e. gained: mgΔh = ½mv².<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So v = √(2gΔh); the mass cancels.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p6-fac-energy-may-be-stored-as-ki","phys-facts",6,"Name the energy stores.","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Energy may be stored as kinetic, gravitational potential, chemical, elastic (strain), nuclear, electrostatic and internal (thermal).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Energy is measured in joules (J).</p></div>",0,""],["n-p7-exp-power-station-boiler-turbi","phys-expl",7,"Power station: boiler, turbine and generator","<p>In a <strong>fossil-fuel, biofuel or nuclear</strong> power station, the fuel heats water in a <strong>boiler</strong> to make <strong>steam</strong>; the steam turns a <strong>turbine</strong>; the turbine turns a <strong>generator</strong>, which produces electricity. Hydroelectric, tidal, wave and wind stations turn the turbine directly, with no boiler.</p>",0,""],["n-p7-exp-water-cycle-and-hydroelect","phys-expl",7,"Water cycle and hydroelectric power","<p>The Sun evaporates water; it falls as rain and fills reservoirs behind <strong>dams</strong>. The water's <strong>gravitational potential energy</strong> turns turbines as it flows down. Without the water cycle (driven by the Sun) there would be no hydroelectric power.</p>",1,""],["n-p7-law-comparing-energy-resources","phys-law",7,"Comparing energy resources","<p>Judge each resource by <strong>renewability</strong>, <strong>availability</strong>, <strong>reliability</strong>, <strong>scale</strong> (how much power it can supply, e.g. for a large population), <strong>environmental impact</strong> (climate, land, habitats, pollution) and <strong>cost</strong> (start-up and running costs).</p>",0,""],["n-p7-dif-how-the-energy-resources-c","phys-diff",7,"How the energy resources compare","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Resource</th><th>How it is used</th><th>Renewable?</th><th>Reliable?</th><th>Environmental impact</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Fossil fuels</td><td>Burned → boiler → turbine → generator</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, but long start-up time</td><td>CO₂ (global warming), SO₂ (acid rain)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Biofuels</td><td>Burned → boiler → turbine → generator</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>CO₂ released, but the plants absorbed CO₂ while growing; land use</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Nuclear (fission)</td><td>Fission heats boiler → turbine → generator</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td><td>No CO₂; radioactive waste; risk of accidents</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hydroelectric</td><td>Water from a dam turns turbines</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes; very fast start-up</td><td>No CO₂; floods valleys and habitats</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tidal</td><td>Tides flow through a barrage, turning turbines</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, predictable</td><td>No CO₂; damages estuaries</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Waves</td><td>Up-and-down motion of waves drives generators</td><td>Yes</td><td>No, depends on weather</td><td>No CO₂; hazard to boats; expensive</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wind</td><td>Wind turns turbine blades</td><td>Yes</td><td>No, depends on wind</td><td>No CO₂; noisy; spoils landscape</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Geothermal</td><td>Hot rocks make steam to turn turbines</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Very little; only in some places</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solar cells</td><td>Light → electricity directly</td><td>Yes</td><td>No, daylight only</td><td>No CO₂ when running; large area needed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Solar panels</td><td>Infrared heats water</td><td>Yes</td><td>No, daylight only</td><td>No CO₂ when running</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p7-dif-wave-power-vs-hydroelectri","phys-diff",7,"Wave power vs hydroelectric power","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Wave power</th><th>Hydroelectric power</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Where</td><td>Sea and ocean</td><td>Rivers, with a dam</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reliability</td><td><strong>Unreliable</strong> (depends on the weather)</td><td><strong>Reliable</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Start-up time</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Very short</strong>, so useful when demand suddenly rises</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy store used</td><td>Kinetic energy of waves</td><td><strong>Gravitational potential energy</strong> of water behind the dam</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Problem</td><td>Hard to convert up-and-down motion into spinning motion; machines corrode and are damaged in storms</td><td>Floods land; expensive to build</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p7-dif-fossil-fuels-vs-biofuels","phys-diff",7,"Fossil fuels vs biofuels","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fossil fuels</th><th>Biofuels</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Oil, coal, natural gas</td><td>Wood, animal dung, ethanol, biogas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Renewable?</td><td><strong>No</strong></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect on climate</td><td>Release CO₂ → global warming and climate change</td><td>Release CO₂ when burned, but plants absorbed it as they grew, so the overall effect is much smaller</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Other pollution</td><td>SO₂ → acid rain, damage to ecosystems and buildings</td><td>Smoke → respiratory health problems</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p7-fac-burning-coal-and-oil-produ","phys-facts",7,"Which gas from burning coal and oil causes acid rain?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Burning coal and oil produces sulfur dioxide (SO₂), which causes acid rain.</p>",0,""],["n-p7-fac-coal-releases-more-co-than","phys-facts",7,"Which fossil fuel releases the most CO₂ for the same energy?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Coal releases more CO₂ than oil or natural gas for the same energy.</p>",0,""],["n-p7-fac-burning-biofuels-such-as-w","phys-facts",7,"Health problem from burning wood and dung?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Burning biofuels such as wood and dung gives off smoke, which causes respiratory and health problems.</p>",0,""],["n-p7-fac-resources-that-produce-no","phys-facts",7,"Which energy resources produce no CO₂ while generating?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Resources that produce no CO₂ while generating: hydroelectric, tidal, wave, wind, geothermal, solar and nuclear.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Biomass (biofuel) <strong>does</strong> release CO₂ when burned.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p7-fac-wave-power-is-expensive-be","phys-facts",7,"Why is wave power expensive?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Wave power is expensive because the machines corrode in salt water and are damaged in storms.</p>",0,""],["n-p7-fac-light-and-thermal-radiatio","phys-facts",7,"How long does light take to travel from the Sun to the Earth?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Light and thermal radiation take about 8 minutes to travel from the Sun to the Earth.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>See Ch 24 for the calculation.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p7-fac-radiation-from-the-sun-is","phys-facts",7,"The Sun is the main source of energy for all our energy resources except…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\"><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Radiation from the Sun is the main source of energy for all our energy resources <strong>except geothermal, nuclear and tidal</strong>.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Fossil fuels and biofuels came from plants (photosynthesis); wind and waves come from the Sun heating the air; hydroelectric depends on the water cycle.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p7-fac-research-is-being-carried","phys-facts",7,"What research is being done into nuclear fusion?","<p class=\"qn-lead\"><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Research is being carried out (e.g. tokamak reactors, sometimes called an \"artificial sun\") into using <strong>nuclear fusion</strong> to produce electrical energy on a large scale.</p>",1,""],["n-p8-for-work-done","phys-form",8,"Work done","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">W = Fd = ΔE</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>W</b> = work done (J)</li><li><b>F</b> = force (N)</li><li><b>d</b> = distance moved in the direction of the force (m)</li><li><b>ΔE</b> = energy transferred (J)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Lifting an object: W = mgΔh, the gain in g.p.e.</p>",0,""],["n-p8-for-power","phys-form",8,"Power","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">P = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">W</span><span class=\"de\">t</span></span>   and   P = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">ΔE</span><span class=\"de\">t</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>P</b> = power (W)</li><li><b>W</b> = work done (J)</li><li><b>ΔE</b> = energy transferred (J)</li><li><b>t</b> = time (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>W = Pt</b> · <b>t = W ÷ P</b></p>",0,""],["n-p8-for-efficiency-power","phys-form",8,"Efficiency (power)","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">% efficiency = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">useful power output</span><span class=\"de\">total power input</span></span> × 100%</div>",1,""],["n-p8-uni-work-energy-and-power-unit","phys-unit",8,"Work, energy and power units","<p><strong>1 J = 1 N m</strong>. 1 kJ = 1000 J; 1 MJ = 1 000 000 J. <strong>1 W = 1 J/s</strong>; <strong>1 kW = 1000 W</strong>; <strong>1 MW = 1 000 000 W</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p8-fac-to-calculate-the-energy-tr","phys-facts",8,"Work done against gravity: which distance do you use?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">To calculate the energy transferred by the force of gravity, use the <strong>vertical height</strong> moved.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. walking up a ramp: work against gravity = mg × vertical height, not × the length of the ramp.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p8-fac-a-more-powerful-machine-do","phys-facts",8,"What does a more powerful machine do?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A more powerful machine does the same work in less time.</p>",0,""],["n-p9-exp-states-of-matter","phys-expl",9,"States of matter","<p><strong>Solid:</strong> fixed shape and fixed volume. <strong>Liquid:</strong> fixed volume, takes the shape of its container. <strong>Gas:</strong> no fixed shape or volume; fills its container and can be compressed. Changes of state: <strong>melting</strong> (solid → liquid), <strong>boiling/evaporation</strong> (liquid → gas), <strong>condensation</strong> (gas → liquid), <strong>solidification/freezing</strong> (liquid → solid).</p>",0,""],["n-p9-exp-particle-model-of-solids-l","phys-expl",9,"Particle model of solids, liquids and gases","<p><strong>Solid:</strong> particles close together in a regular pattern; strong forces; they <strong>vibrate</strong> about fixed positions. <strong>Liquid:</strong> close together, irregular; they <strong>move around</strong> past each other. <strong>Gas:</strong> far apart, random; weak forces; they move <strong>quickly in all directions</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> The forces and distances between particles, and their motion, explain these properties (e.g. gases are easy to compress because the particles are far apart).</p>",1,""],["n-p9-exp-gas-pressure","phys-expl",9,"Gas pressure","<p>Gas particles move randomly and <strong>collide with the walls</strong> of the container. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Each collision exerts a <strong>force</strong>; the total force per unit area of wall is the <strong>pressure</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-p9-for-kelvin-and-celsius","phys-form",9,"Kelvin and Celsius","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">T (in K) = θ (in °C) + 273</div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>θ = T − 273</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. 27 °C = 300 K; 0 K = −273 °C.</p>",0,""],["n-p9-for-pressure-and-volume-of-a-g","phys-form",9,"Pressure and volume of a gas","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">pV = constant,   so   p₁V₁ = p₂V₂</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>p</b> = pressure (Pa)</li><li><b>V</b> = volume (m³ or cm³)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Only for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature. Use the same units on both sides.</p>",1,""],["n-p9-uni-pressure-units","phys-unit",9,"Pressure units","<p><strong>1 Pa = 1 N/m²</strong>. Atmospheric pressure ≈ 100 kPa (about 101 kPa).</p>",0,""],["n-p9-law-kinetic-particle-model-tem","phys-law",9,"Kinetic particle model (temperature and particle motion)","<p><strong>As the temperature of a substance rises, its particles gain kinetic energy and move faster.</strong> At absolute zero (0 K = −273 °C) they have the least kinetic energy.</p>",0,""],["n-p9-law-gas-pressure-and-temperatu","phys-law",9,"Gas pressure and temperature (constant volume)","<p><strong>When a fixed mass of gas is heated at constant volume, its pressure increases</strong>, because the particles move faster, so they hit the walls <strong>more often</strong> and <strong>harder</strong> (with more force).</p>",0,""],["n-p9-law-boyle-s-law-volume-and-pre","phys-law",9,"Boyle's law (volume and pressure)","<p><strong>For a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, pressure is inversely proportional to volume (pV = constant).</strong> If the volume is made smaller, the particles hit the walls <strong>more often</strong> (the same particles in less space), so the pressure increases.</p>",1,""],["n-p9-dif-celsius-scale-vs-kelvin-sc","phys-diff",9,"Celsius scale vs Kelvin scale","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Celsius (°C)</th><th>Kelvin (K)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Zero point</td><td>0 °C = melting point of ice</td><td><strong>0 K = absolute zero (−273 °C)</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fixed points</td><td>0 °C (ice melts), 100 °C (water boils)</td><td>273 K and 373 K</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Negative values?</td><td>Yes, below 0 °C</td><td><strong>Never negative</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Size of one degree</td><td>A change of 1 °C…</td><td>…= a change of 1 K</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Used for</td><td>Everyday temperatures</td><td>Science, gas calculations</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p9-dif-solids-vs-liquids-vs-gases","phys-diff",9,"Solids vs liquids vs gases","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Solid</th><th>Liquid</th><th>Gas</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Takes shape of container</td><td>Fills container</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Volume</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Fixed</td><td>Not fixed (compressible)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arrangement</td><td>Regular, close together</td><td>Irregular, close together</td><td>Random, far apart</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Motion</td><td>Vibrate about fixed positions</td><td>Move past each other</td><td>Move quickly in all directions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Forces between particles</td><td>Strong</td><td>Weaker</td><td>Very weak</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p9-fac-molecules-in-a-hot-gas-mov","phys-facts",9,"Molecules in a hot gas compared with a cold gas?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Molecules in a hot gas move faster than in a cold gas.</p>",0,""],["n-p9-fac-internal-energy-is-the-tot","phys-facts",9,"What is internal energy?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Internal energy is the total kinetic and potential energy of all the particles in an object.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>A rise in temperature increases internal energy.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p9-fac-the-temperature-of-an-obje","phys-facts",9,"What does the temperature of an object measure?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The temperature of an object is a measure of the average kinetic energy of its particles.</p>",0,""],["n-p9-fac-at-absolute-zero-the-parti","phys-facts",9,"What happens to particles at absolute zero?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">At absolute zero the particles have the least possible kinetic energy, so the substance cannot be cooled any further.</p>",0,""],["n-p9-fac-absolute-zero-is-273-c-0-k","phys-facts",9,"Absolute zero in °C and in K?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Absolute zero is −273 °C (0 K).</p>",0,""],["n-p10-exp-melting-and-boiling","phys-expl",10,"Melting and boiling","<p>During <strong>melting</strong> and <strong>boiling</strong>, energy is supplied but the <strong>temperature does not change</strong>: the energy is used to break the forces between particles. Water melts at <strong>0 °C</strong> and boils at <strong>100 °C</strong> at standard atmospheric pressure.</p>",0,""],["n-p10-exp-condensation-and-solidific","phys-expl",10,"Condensation and solidification","<p><strong>Condensation:</strong> gas particles slow down and come close together, forming a liquid; energy is released. <strong>Solidification:</strong> liquid particles slow down and settle into fixed positions in a regular pattern.</p>",0,""],["n-p10-exp-cooling-by-evaporation","phys-expl",10,"Cooling by evaporation","<p>The <strong>more energetic</strong> (faster) particles escape from the surface, so the <strong>average kinetic energy of the particles left behind falls</strong>: the temperature of the liquid falls. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> An object in contact with the evaporating liquid (e.g. skin with sweat) is cooled, because thermal energy passes from the object to the liquid.</p>",1,""],["n-p10-exp-order-of-expansion","phys-expl",10,"Order of expansion","<p><strong>Gases expand most, then liquids, then solids.</strong> In a <strong>solid</strong> the particles are held tightly by strong forces, so extra vibration only pushes them slightly apart. In a <strong>liquid</strong> the forces are weaker, so they move further apart. In a <strong>gas</strong> the forces are negligible, so the particles move much further apart.</p>",1,""],["n-p10-exp-applications-and-consequen","phys-expl",10,"Applications and consequences of thermal expansion","<p>Gaps in railway tracks and expansion joints in bridges; overhead cables sag in summer; liquid-in-glass thermometers; bimetallic strips in thermostats and alarms; loosening a tight metal lid by heating it.</p>",0,""],["n-p10-for-specific-heat-capacity","phys-form",10,"Specific heat capacity","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">c = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">ΔE</span><span class=\"de\">mΔθ</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>c</b> = specific heat capacity (J/(kg °C))</li><li><b>ΔE</b> = energy supplied (J)</li><li><b>m</b> = mass (kg)</li><li><b>Δθ</b> = temperature rise (°C)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>ΔE = mcΔθ</b> · <b>Δθ = ΔE ÷ (mc)</b></p>",1,""],["n-p10-law-factors-that-speed-up-evap","phys-law",10,"Factors that speed up evaporation","<p><strong>Evaporation is faster at a higher temperature, with a larger surface area, and with air moving over the surface.</strong> Higher temperature: more particles have enough energy to escape. Larger area: more particles are at the surface. Moving air: removes escaped particles before they can return.</p>",1,""],["n-p10-dif-evaporation-vs-boiling","phys-diff",10,"Evaporation vs boiling","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Evaporation</th><th>Boiling</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Temperature</td><td><strong>Any temperature</strong></td><td>Only at the <strong>boiling point</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where it happens</td><td><strong>Only at the surface</strong></td><td><strong>Throughout</strong> the liquid</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bubbles</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temperature of the liquid</td><td>Falls (it cools)</td><td>Stays constant while energy is supplied</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy</td><td>Taken from the liquid (and surroundings)</td><td>Must be supplied by heating</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-p10-exp-specific-heat-capacity-of","phys-exp",10,"Specific heat capacity of a solid","<p>Use an aluminium block (mass m) with holes for an <strong>electric heater</strong> and a <strong>thermometer</strong>; wrap it in insulation. Record the starting temperature. Switch on for time t, recording V and I (or use a joulemeter). Record the highest temperature. <strong>ΔE = VIt</strong>; <strong>c = ΔE ÷ (mΔθ)</strong>. The result is too high because some energy is lost to the surroundings; insulation reduces this.</p>",1,""],["n-p10-exp-specific-heat-capacity-of-2","phys-exp",10,"Specific heat capacity of a liquid","<p>Same method with a known mass of <strong>water</strong> in an insulated beaker (or polystyrene cup), heated by an immersion heater; <strong>stir</strong> before reading the temperature.</p>",1,""],["n-p10-fac-uses-of-a-bimetallic-strip","phys-facts",10,"Uses of a bimetallic strip?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Uses of a bimetallic strip: thermostats (switching electrical circuits), fire alarms and frost alarms.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The metal that expands more ends up on the outside of the bend.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p10-fac-expansion-on-heating-gas-l","phys-facts",10,"Order of expansion on heating: solid, liquid, gas?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Expansion on heating: gas &gt; liquid &gt; solid.</p>",0,""],["n-p10-fac-a-rise-in-the-temperature","phys-facts",10,"What does a rise in temperature do to an object&#x27;s internal energy?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A rise in the temperature of an object increases its internal energy.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> It increases the <strong>average kinetic energy of all the particles</strong> in the object.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p11-exp-conduction-in-solids","phys-expl",11,"Conduction in solids","<p>In <strong>all solids</strong>, the particles at the hot end vibrate more and pass the vibrations on to neighbouring particles (<strong>lattice vibrations</strong>). In <strong>metals</strong> there are also <strong>free (delocalised) electrons</strong> that move quickly through the metal and carry energy, which is why metals are much better conductors.</p>",1,""],["n-p11-exp-why-conduction-is-bad-in-l","phys-expl",11,"Why conduction is bad in liquids and gases","<p>The particles are <strong>further apart</strong>, so collisions that pass on energy are less frequent; there are no free electrons. Gases are the worst conductors because their particles are furthest apart.</p>",1,""],["n-p11-exp-surface-colour-and-texture","phys-expl",11,"Surface colour and texture","<p><strong>Dull (matt) black</strong> surfaces are the <strong>best emitters and best absorbers</strong> of infrared. <strong>Shiny white or silver</strong> surfaces are the <strong>worst emitters and absorbers</strong>; they are the <strong>best reflectors</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p11-exp-temperature-of-the-earth","phys-expl",11,"Temperature of the Earth","<p>The Earth's temperature depends on the balance between <strong>incoming radiation</strong> from the Sun and <strong>radiation emitted</strong> from the Earth's surface. Greenhouse gases (e.g. CO₂) absorb some of the emitted infrared, so less escapes and the temperature rises.</p>",1,""],["n-p11-exp-rate-of-emission","phys-expl",11,"Rate of emission","<p>An object emits radiation <strong>faster</strong> if its <strong>surface temperature is higher</strong> and its <strong>surface area is larger</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-p11-exp-everyday-applications","phys-expl",11,"Everyday applications","<p><strong>Kitchen pan:</strong> metal base conducts thermal energy to the food; plastic or wooden handle is an insulator. <strong>Heating a room:</strong> a heater warms the air next to it, which rises, and cooler air sinks to replace it (convection current). <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Fire burning wood or coal:</strong> radiation from the flames warms you; convection carries hot gases up the chimney and draws in fresh air. <strong>Car radiator:</strong> hot coolant from the engine is cooled by conduction through the thin metal fins, convection of the air passing over them, and radiation.</p>",1,""],["n-p11-exp-good-and-bad-thermal-condu","phys-exp",11,"Good and bad thermal conductors","<p>Take rods of the <strong>same size</strong> made of different materials (copper, aluminium, steel, glass, wood). Fix a drawing pin with wax at the same distance along each. Heat one end of all the rods equally. The pin falls off first from the <strong>best conductor</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p11-exp-convection-in-a-liquid","phys-exp",11,"Convection in a liquid","<p>Drop a crystal of <strong>potassium manganate(VII)</strong> at the bottom of a beaker of water, near one side. Heat gently under the crystal. The purple colour rises above the flame, moves across the top and sinks on the other side, showing the convection current. (In air: smoke from a smouldering straw shows the air flow in a box with a candle under one chimney.)</p>",0,""],["n-p11-exp-good-and-bad-emitters","phys-exp",11,"Good and bad emitters","<p>Fill a <strong>Leslie's cube</strong> (sides: dull black, shiny silver, white, dull grey) with hot water. Hold an infrared detector (or thermometer) the <strong>same distance</strong> from each side. The dull black side gives the highest reading. (Or: compare the cooling of two identical cans of hot water, one dull black and one shiny.)</p>",1,""],["n-p11-exp-good-and-bad-absorbers","phys-exp",11,"Good and bad absorbers","<p>Place two identical metal plates (one dull black, one shiny) the <strong>same distance</strong> from a radiant heater, each with a drawing pin stuck on the back with wax. The pin on the <strong>dull black</strong> plate falls first. (Or: two thermometers with blackened and shiny bulbs.)</p>",1,""],["n-p11-fac-thermal-conductors-from-be","phys-facts",11,"Thermal conductors, from best to worst?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Thermal conductors, from best to worst: diamond; silver, copper; aluminium, steel; lead; ice, marble, glass; polythene, nylon; rubber, wood; polystyrene; glass wool (a very good insulator).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Many solids conduct better than insulators but less well than metals.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p11-fac-dull-matt-black-surfaces-a","phys-facts",11,"Best absorbers of infrared radiation?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dull (matt) black surfaces are the best absorbers of infrared radiation.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Absorption depends on the <strong>surface</strong> (colour and texture), not on the material being plastic.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p11-fac-dull-matt-black-surfaces-a-2","phys-facts",11,"Best and worst emitters of infrared radiation?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Dull (matt) black surfaces are also the best emitters; shiny silver surfaces are the worst emitters.</p>",0,""],["n-p11-fac-infrared-radiation-does-no","phys-facts",11,"Does infrared radiation need a medium?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Infrared radiation does not need a medium.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>So it travels through a vacuum, e.g. from the Sun.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p11-fac-thermal-radiation-transfer","phys-facts",11,"How does thermal radiation transfer energy?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Thermal radiation transfers energy as electromagnetic waves (infrared).</p>",0,""],["n-p11-fac-cold-objects-also-cause-co","phys-facts",11,"Can cold objects cause convection currents?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Cold objects also cause convection currents.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>e.g. air cooled by a freezer compartment is denser and sinks.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p11-fac-for-an-object-to-be-at-a-c","phys-facts",11,"Condition for an object to stay at a constant temperature?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">For an object to be at a constant temperature, it must transfer energy away at the same rate that it receives energy.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>If it receives energy <strong>faster</strong> than it loses it, its temperature <strong>rises</strong>; if more slowly, its temperature <strong>falls</strong>.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p12-exp-production-of-sound","phys-expl",12,"Production of sound","<p>Sound is produced by <strong>vibrating</strong> sources (a guitar string, a loudspeaker cone, vocal cords). The vibrations make the particles of the medium vibrate back and forth, so sound is a <strong>longitudinal</strong> wave. Sound <strong>needs a medium</strong> (solid, liquid or gas); it cannot travel through a vacuum.</p>",0,""],["n-p12-exp-oscilloscope-cro","phys-expl",12,"Oscilloscope (CRO)","<p>Shows a sound wave as a trace on a screen, so you can \"see\" a sound. The <strong>height</strong> of the trace shows the <strong>amplitude</strong> (loudness); the <strong>number of waves</strong> across the screen shows the <strong>frequency</strong> (pitch).</p>",0,""],["n-p12-exp-uses-of-ultrasound","phys-expl",12,"Uses of ultrasound","<p><strong>Non-destructive testing</strong> of materials (finding cracks inside metal), <strong>medical scanning of soft tissue</strong> (e.g. a fetus; safer than X-rays), and <strong>sonar</strong> (finding the depth of the sea or locating fish and submarines). A pulse is sent out; the echo returns after time t. <strong>Distance = speed × time ÷ 2</strong>, because the pulse travels there and back.</p>",1,""],["n-p12-for-speed-of-sound","phys-form",12,"Speed of sound","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">speed = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">distance</span><span class=\"de\">time</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li>speed (m/s)</li><li>distance (m)</li><li>time (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">For an echo the sound goes there and back: distance to the wall (or depth) = speed × time ÷ 2.</p>",0,""],["n-p12-uni-hertz","phys-unit",12,"Hertz","<p><strong>1 Hz = 1 wave per second</strong> (= 1/s). 1 kHz = 1000 Hz.</p>",0,""],["n-p12-exp-measuring-the-speed-of-sou","phys-exp",12,"Measuring the speed of sound in air","<p>Two people stand a measured distance apart (at least 100 m; use a tape measure or trundle wheel). One bangs two blocks of wood (or cymbals) together; the other <strong>starts a stop-watch on seeing</strong> the action and <strong>stops it on hearing</strong> the sound (light arrives almost instantly). Speed = distance ÷ time. Repeat and take the <strong>mean</strong> to reduce reaction-time errors. (Echo method: stand a measured distance from a large wall, clap and time the echo; speed = 2 × distance ÷ time.)</p>",0,""],["n-p12-fac-larger-amplitude-louder-so","phys-facts",12,"What does a larger amplitude do to a sound?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Larger amplitude = louder sound.</p>",0,""],["n-p12-fac-higher-frequency-higher-pi","phys-facts",12,"What does a higher frequency do to a sound?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Higher frequency = higher pitch.</p>",0,""],["n-p12-fac-the-range-of-human-hearing","phys-facts",12,"Range of human hearing?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The range of human hearing is about 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz (20 kHz).</p>",0,""],["n-p12-fac-sound-travels-about-1-km-i","phys-facts",12,"Speed of sound in air?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sound travels about 1 km in 3 s in air: the speed of sound in air is about 330–350 m/s.</p>",0,""],["n-p12-fac-light-travels-at-300-000-0","phys-facts",12,"Speed of light, compared with sound?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Light travels at 300 000 000 m/s (3.0 × 10⁸ m/s), about a million times faster than sound.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>This is why you see lightning before you hear thunder.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p12-fac-sound-travels-faster-in-so","phys-facts",12,"Speed of sound in solids, liquids and gases: order?","<p class=\"qn-lead\"><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Sound travels faster in solids than in liquids, and faster in liquids than in gases.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The particles are closer together.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p13-exp-image-in-a-plane-mirror","phys-expl",13,"Image in a plane mirror","<p>The image is the <strong>same size</strong> as the object, the <strong>same distance behind</strong> the mirror as the object is in front, <strong>virtual</strong> (cannot be formed on a screen) and <strong>upright</strong>, but laterally inverted (left and right swapped).</p>",0,""],["n-p13-exp-optical-fibres","phys-expl",13,"Optical fibres","<p>Light (visible or infrared) travels along a thin glass fibre by <strong>repeated total internal reflection</strong>, with very little loss. Used in <strong>telecommunications</strong> (telephone, cable TV and high-speed broadband), and in endoscopes to see inside the body.</p>",1,""],["n-p13-exp-converging-and-diverging-l","phys-expl",13,"Converging and diverging lenses","<p>A <strong>converging</strong> (convex) lens bends a parallel beam of light <strong>inwards</strong> so it meets at the <strong>principal focus</strong>. A <strong>diverging</strong> (concave) lens spreads a parallel beam <strong>outwards</strong>, as if it came from a principal focus on the same side as the light.</p>",0,""],["n-p13-exp-describing-an-image","phys-expl",13,"Describing an image","<p>Use three words: <strong>enlarged / same size / diminished</strong>; <strong>upright / inverted</strong>; <strong>real / virtual</strong>. A <strong>virtual</strong> image is formed where <strong>diverging rays are extrapolated backwards</strong>; it does not form a visible projection on a screen.</p>",0,""],["n-p13-exp-magnifying-glass","phys-expl",13,"Magnifying glass","<p>A single converging lens with the object <strong>closer than the principal focus</strong>. The image is <strong>virtual, upright and enlarged</strong>, on the same side of the lens as the object.</p>",1,""],["n-p13-for-law-of-reflection","phys-form",13,"Law of reflection","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">i = r</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>i</b> = angle of incidence</li><li><b>r</b> = angle of reflection (both measured from the normal)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Your item 27 says the same thing in words.</p>",0,""],["n-p13-for-refractive-index-from-angl","phys-form",13,"Refractive index from angles","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">n = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">sin i</span><span class=\"de\">sin r</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>n</b> = refractive index (no unit)</li><li><b>i</b> = angle of incidence in air</li><li><b>r</b> = angle of refraction in the material</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>sin r = sin i ÷ n</b></p>",1,""],["n-p13-for-refractive-index-from-crit","phys-form",13,"Refractive index from critical angle","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">n = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">sin c</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>c</b> = critical angle</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>sin c = 1 ÷ n</b></p>",1,""],["n-p13-law-law-of-reflection","phys-law",13,"Law of reflection","<p><strong>The angle of incidence is equal to the angle of reflection.</strong> Both angles are measured from the normal. It applies to all mirrors, flat or curved.</p>",0,""],["n-p13-dif-real-image-vs-virtual-imag","phys-diff",13,"Real image vs virtual image","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Real image</th><th>Virtual image</th></tr>\n<tr><td>On a screen?</td><td><strong>Can be formed on a screen</strong></td><td><strong>Cannot</strong> be formed on a screen</td></tr>\n<tr><td>How it forms</td><td>Light rays actually meet</td><td>Rays only <strong>appear</strong> to come from it (extrapolated backwards)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Usually</td><td>Inverted</td><td>Upright</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Formed by</td><td>Converging lens with the object <strong>beyond F</strong> (e.g. camera, projector)</td><td><strong>Plane mirror</strong>; converging lens with the object <strong>closer than F</strong> (magnifying glass); diverging lens</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p13-dif-short-sight-vs-long-sight","phys-diff",13,"Short sight vs long sight","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Short sight</th><th>Long sight</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Can't see clearly</td><td><strong>Distant</strong> objects</td><td><strong>Near</strong> objects</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cause</td><td>Eyeball too long, or eye lens too strong</td><td>Eyeball too short, or eye lens too weak</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Where the image forms</td><td><strong>In front of</strong> the retina</td><td><strong>Behind</strong> the retina</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Corrected by</td><td><strong>Diverging</strong> lens</td><td><strong>Converging</strong> lens</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-p13-exp-refraction-of-light-by-a-g","phys-exp",13,"Refraction of light by a glass block","<p>Place a rectangular glass (or Perspex) block on paper and draw round it. Shine a ray from a <strong>ray box</strong> at the block (or use <strong>optics pins</strong>). Mark the incident and emergent rays with small crosses; remove the block and join them up. Draw the <strong>normal</strong> where the ray enters; measure <strong>i</strong> and <strong>r</strong> with a protractor. Repeat for different angles of incidence. Use a <strong>semicircular block</strong> (ray aimed at the centre of the flat side) to find the critical angle and see total internal reflection.</p>",0,""],["n-p13-fac-long-sight-is-corrected-by","phys-facts",13,"Long sight is corrected by which lens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Long sight is corrected by a converging lens.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p13-fac-short-sight-is-corrected-b","phys-facts",13,"Short sight is corrected by which lens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Short sight is corrected by a diverging lens.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p13-fac-a-magnifying-glass-produce","phys-facts",13,"Image formed by a magnifying glass?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A magnifying glass produces a virtual, upright, enlarged image.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p13-fac-a-diverging-lens-is-thinne","phys-facts",13,"Shape of a diverging lens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A diverging lens is thinner in the middle than at the edges.</p>",0,""],["n-p13-fac-a-converging-lens-is-fatte","phys-facts",13,"Shape of a converging lens?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A converging lens is fatter in the middle than at the edges.</p>",0,""],["n-p13-fac-the-seven-colours-of-the-v","phys-facts",13,"The colours of the visible spectrum, in order of decreasing wavelength?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The seven colours of the visible spectrum, in order of decreasing wavelength (increasing frequency): red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Red: longest wavelength, lowest frequency. Violet: shortest wavelength, highest frequency.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p14-exp-reflection-and-refraction","phys-expl",14,"Reflection and refraction of waves","<p><strong>Reflection</strong> at a plane surface: angle of incidence = angle of reflection; speed, frequency and wavelength unchanged. <strong>Refraction</strong> is due to a <strong>change of speed</strong>: in a ripple tank, waves slow down in shallower water, so the <strong>wavelength gets shorter</strong> (frequency stays the same) and, if they arrive at an angle, they <strong>change direction</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p14-exp-how-gap-size-and-wavelengt","phys-expl",14,"How gap size and wavelength affect diffraction","<p>Diffraction is <strong>greatest when the gap is about the same size as the wavelength</strong>. A gap much wider than the wavelength gives very little spreading. At an <strong>edge</strong>, waves with a <strong>longer wavelength</strong> diffract (bend round) more. This is why you can hear round a corner (sound has a long wavelength) but cannot see round it (light has a very short wavelength).</p>",1,""],["n-p14-for-wave-speed","phys-form",14,"Wave speed","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">v = fλ</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>v</b> = wave speed (m/s)</li><li><b>f</b> = frequency (Hz)</li><li><b>λ</b> = wavelength (m)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>f = v ÷ λ</b> · <b>λ = v ÷ f</b></p>",0,""],["n-p14-for-frequency-and-period","phys-form",14,"Frequency and period","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">f = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">T</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>f</b> = frequency (Hz)</li><li><b>T</b> = period, the time for one complete wave (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Not in the syllabus list, but useful for oscilloscope traces and a.c. graphs.</p>",0,""],["n-p14-uni-hertz","phys-unit",14,"Hertz","<p><strong>1 Hz = 1/s</strong> (one wave per second). See also Ch 12.</p>",0,""],["n-p14-dif-transverse-waves-vs-longit","phys-diff",14,"Transverse waves vs longitudinal waves","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Transverse</th><th>Longitudinal</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Direction of vibration</td><td><strong>At right angles (perpendicular)</strong> to the direction of travel (side to side)</td><td><strong>Parallel</strong> to the direction of travel (back and forth)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Features</td><td>Crests and troughs</td><td>Compressions and rarefactions</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td><strong>Electromagnetic waves, water waves, seismic S-waves</strong></td><td><strong>Sound waves, seismic P-waves</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Model</td><td>A rope shaken up and down</td><td>A slinky spring pushed and pulled along its length</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p14-exp-using-a-ripple-tank","phys-exp",14,"Using a ripple tank","<p>A shallow tray of water with a <strong>vibrating bar (dipper)</strong> makes straight (plane) waves; a <strong>lamp above</strong> casts shadows of the wavefronts on a screen below. (a) <strong>Reflection:</strong> put a straight barrier in the water at an angle. (b) <strong>Refraction:</strong> put a glass plate on the bottom to make a shallow region; waves slow down, the wavelength shortens and the direction changes. (c) <strong>Diffraction through a gap:</strong> two barriers with a gap between them. (d) <strong>Diffraction at an edge:</strong> one barrier; waves curve round its end.</p>",0,""],["n-p14-fac-seismic-waves-from-earthqu","phys-facts",14,"P-waves and S-waves: which is faster, and which type of wave is each?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Seismic waves from earthquakes: P-waves (primary) are faster, longitudinal waves; S-waves (secondary) are slower, transverse waves.</p>",0,""],["n-p14-fac-sound-is-a-longitudinal-wa","phys-facts",14,"Sound: type of wave and frequency range?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Sound is a longitudinal wave with frequencies from about 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz.</p>",0,""],["n-p14-fac-waves-transfer-energy-with","phys-facts",14,"What do waves transfer, and what do they not transfer?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Waves transfer energy without transferring matter.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Wave motion can be seen in vibrations of ropes and springs and in water waves (ripple tank): the medium only vibrates about a fixed position.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p15-exp-benefits-of-digital-signal","phys-expl",15,"Benefits of digital signals","<p>Sound can be sent as an analogue or a digital signal. Digital is better because it allows an <strong>increased rate of transmission of data</strong>, and an <strong>increased range</strong>, because the signal can be <strong>accurately regenerated</strong>: noise picked up along the way is removed, since each pulse only has to be recognised as on or off. Analogue signals get distorted, because the noise is amplified along with the signal.</p>",1,""],["n-p15-dif-analogue-signal-vs-digital","phys-diff",15,"Analogue signal vs digital signal","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Analogue</th><th>Digital</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Form</td><td>Varies <strong>continuously</strong></td><td><strong>Pulses</strong>, only two values (on/off, 1/0)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effect of noise</td><td>Noise is amplified with the signal → <strong>distorted</strong>, not clear</td><td>Signal <strong>regenerated accurately</strong> → clear</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Data rate</td><td>Lower</td><td><strong>Higher</strong> (faster, more data)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Range</td><td>Shorter</td><td><strong>Longer</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-p15-dif-uses-and-dangers-of-each-r","phys-diff",15,"Uses and dangers of each region","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Region</th><th>Typical uses</th><th>Harmful effects of too much exposure</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Radio waves</td><td>Radio and television transmissions, astronomy, RFID (radio frequency identification)</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Microwaves</td><td>Satellite television, mobile phones, microwave ovens</td><td><strong>Internal heating of body cells</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Infrared</td><td>Electric grills, remote controls (short-range communication), intruder alarms, thermal imaging, optical fibres</td><td><strong>Skin burns</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Visible light</td><td>Vision, photography, illumination</td><td>—</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ultraviolet</td><td>Security marking, detecting fake bank notes, sterilising water</td><td><strong>Damage to surface cells and eyes → skin cancer and eye conditions</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>X-rays</td><td>Medical scanning, security scanners</td><td><strong>Mutation or damage to cells</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gamma rays</td><td>Sterilising food and medical equipment, detection and treatment of cancer</td><td><strong>Mutation or damage to cells</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p15-fac-communication-systems-use","phys-facts",15,"Which electromagnetic waves are used for communication?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Communication systems use radio waves, microwaves, infrared and visible light.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Satellites:</strong> communication is mainly by <strong>microwaves</strong>; some satellite phones use <strong>low orbit</strong> satellites; others and direct broadcast satellite TV use <strong>geostationary</strong> satellites. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Mobile phones and wireless internet:</strong> microwaves (penetrate some walls, need only a short aerial). <strong>Bluetooth:</strong> radio waves (pass through walls but are weakened). <strong>Optical fibres:</strong> visible light or infrared, for cable TV and high-speed broadband (glass is transparent to them, and they carry high rates of data).</p></div>",1,""],["n-p15-fac-visible-spectrum-roygbiv-v","phys-facts",15,"Memory aid for the order of the visible spectrum?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Visible spectrum: ROYGBIV (VIBGYOR backwards).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Red has the <strong>longest wavelength</strong> and <strong>lowest frequency</strong>; violet the shortest wavelength and highest frequency.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p15-fac-visible-light-has-a-freque","phys-facts",15,"Frequency and wavelength range of visible light?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Visible light has a frequency of about 10¹⁴–10¹⁵ Hz (wavelength about 400–700 nm).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>You only need the <strong>order</strong> of the regions, not these numbers.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p15-fac-typical-wavelengths-from-l","phys-facts",15,"Typical wavelength of each region of the electromagnetic spectrum?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Typical wavelengths, from longest to shortest</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Radio: metres to kilometres; microwaves: about 1 cm; infrared: about 0.01 mm; visible: about 0.0005 mm; ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays: shorter still (gamma shortest). Not required, but it helps to fix the order.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p15-fac-in-order-of-increasing-fre","phys-facts",15,"Electromagnetic spectrum in order of increasing frequency? What do all EM waves share?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">In order of <strong>increasing frequency</strong> (decreasing wavelength): <strong>radio waves, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays, gamma rays.</strong> All electromagnetic waves are transverse and travel at the <strong>same speed in a vacuum</strong>.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> This speed is <strong>3.0 × 10⁸ m/s</strong>, approximately the same in air.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p16-exp-uses-of-magnets","phys-expl",16,"Uses of magnets","<p><strong>Permanent magnets:</strong> compass needles, loudspeakers, motors, fridge doors, magnetic catches. <strong>Electromagnets</strong> (can be switched on and off and their strength changed): scrap-yard cranes, relays, electric bells, circuit breakers, MRI scanners.</p>",0,""],["n-p16-dif-permanent-magnets-hard-ste","phys-diff",16,"Permanent magnets (hard, steel) vs temporary magnets (soft, soft iron)","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Permanent (steel)</th><th>Temporary (soft iron)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Magnetising</td><td>Harder to magnetise</td><td><strong>Easy to magnetise</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Keeping magnetism</td><td><strong>Keeps</strong> its magnetism (hard to demagnetise)</td><td><strong>Loses</strong> its magnetism easily when removed from the field</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Poles</td><td>Fixed poles</td><td>Poles only while in a field</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Uses</td><td>Compass needles, loudspeaker magnets</td><td>Cores of electromagnets, transformers and relays</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p16-exp-plotting-magnetic-field-li","phys-exp",16,"Plotting magnetic field lines","<p><strong>Plotting compass:</strong> place a bar magnet on paper; put a small compass near one pole and mark the direction its N pole points; move the compass so its tail is at the last mark; repeat to build up a field line from N to S. Repeat from other starting points. <strong>Iron filings:</strong> put paper (or a sheet of glass) over the magnet, sprinkle iron filings and tap gently; they line up along the field lines (but don't show direction). Use a compass to find the <strong>direction</strong> of the field.</p>",0,""],["n-p16-fac-the-relative-strength-of-a","phys-facts",16,"How do field lines show the strength of a magnetic field?","<p class=\"qn-lead\"><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> The relative strength of a magnetic field is shown by the spacing of the field lines: closer lines, stronger field.</p>",1,""],["n-p16-fac-a-magnet-attracts-unmagnet","phys-facts",16,"Why does a magnet attract unmagnetised iron at either pole?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A magnet attracts unmagnetised iron at either pole, because it induces an opposite pole in the iron.</p>",0,""],["n-p16-fac-like-poles-repel-unlike-po","phys-facts",16,"Forces between magnetic poles?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Like poles repel; unlike poles attract.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><strong>Repulsion</strong> is the only sure test that an object is a magnet. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Magnetic forces are due to <strong>interactions between magnetic fields</strong>.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p17-exp-electric-field-patterns","phys-expl",17,"Electric field patterns","<p>(a) <strong>Point charge</strong> and (b) <strong>charged conducting sphere:</strong> straight radial lines, outwards for positive, inwards for negative. (c) <strong>Two oppositely charged parallel plates:</strong> evenly spaced, parallel lines from + to −, so the field is uniform (end effects not examined).</p>",1,""],["n-p17-uni-charge","phys-unit",17,"Charge","<p>Charge is measured in <strong>coulombs (C)</strong>. One electron has a charge of −1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C, so 1 C is the charge of about 6 × 10¹⁸ (6 million million million) electrons.</p>",1,""],["n-p17-exp-producing-and-detecting-st","phys-exp",17,"Producing and detecting static charge","<p>Rub a <strong>polythene rod</strong> with a dry cloth (it becomes negative) and an <strong>acetate rod</strong> with a cloth (it becomes positive). Hang one charged rod in a paper stirrup from a thread; bring another charged rod near: <strong>like charges repel, unlike attract</strong>. Detect charge by its ability to pick up small pieces of paper, or with a <strong>gold-leaf electroscope</strong> (the leaf rises when charge is near).</p>",0,""],["n-p17-exp-conductors-and-insulators","phys-exp",17,"Conductors and insulators","<p>Set up a circuit with a cell, a lamp (or ammeter) and two crocodile clips with a gap. Connect each test material across the gap. If the lamp lights (or the ammeter reads), the material is a <strong>conductor</strong>; if not, it is an <strong>insulator</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p17-fac-electrical-conductors-e-g","phys-facts",17,"Conductors and insulators: the difference in terms of electrons?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Electrical conductors (e.g. metals) have free electrons that can move; insulators (e.g. plastic, rubber, glass) do not.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>See Ch 18, items 52–53.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p17-fac-there-are-positive-and-neg","phys-facts",17,"Forces between charges?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">There are positive and negative charges. Like charges repel; unlike charges attract.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Positive repels positive, negative repels negative, positive attracts negative.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p17-fac-charging-a-solid-by-fricti","phys-facts",17,"What moves when a solid is charged by friction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Charging a solid by friction involves only a transfer of negative charge (electrons).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The object that <strong>gains electrons</strong> becomes <strong>negative</strong>; the one that <strong>loses electrons</strong> becomes <strong>positive</strong>. Positive charges (protons) do not move.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p18-exp-charge","phys-expl",18,"Charge","<p>Electric charge can be positive or negative and is measured in <strong>coulombs (C)</strong>. In a metal wire, the charge is carried by <strong>electrons</strong>, which are negative. A current is a flow of charge.</p>",1,""],["n-p18-exp-conduction-in-metals","phys-expl",18,"Conduction in metals","<p>A metal contains <strong>free electrons</strong>. When a p.d. is applied, the free electrons drift through the metal from the negative terminal towards the positive terminal: this is the current.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-exp-resistance-of-a-wire","phys-expl",18,"Resistance of a wire","<p>A <strong>longer</strong> wire has <strong>more</strong> resistance; a <strong>thicker</strong> wire (larger cross-sectional area) has <strong>less</strong> resistance. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Resistance is <strong>directly proportional to length</strong> and <strong>inversely proportional to cross-sectional area</strong>: doubling the length doubles R; doubling the area halves R.</p>",1,""],["n-p18-exp-current-voltage-graphs","phys-expl",18,"Current–voltage graphs","<p><strong>Resistor (constant resistance):</strong> straight line through the origin, current ∝ p.d. <strong>Filament lamp:</strong> a curve that gets less steep, because the filament <strong>gets hotter and its resistance increases</strong>. <strong>Diode:</strong> almost no current in the reverse direction; current flows only above a small forward p.d.</p>",1,""],["n-p18-exp-ammeters-and-voltmeters","phys-expl",18,"Ammeters and voltmeters","<p><strong>Ammeter:</strong> connected <strong>in series</strong>; analogue (needle on a scale) or digital; choose a range bigger than the expected current, then a smaller range for precision. <strong>Voltmeter:</strong> connected <strong>in parallel</strong> across the component.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-for-current-and-charge","phys-form",18,"Current and charge","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">I = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Q</span><span class=\"de\">t</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>I</b> = current (A)</li><li><b>Q</b> = charge (C)</li><li><b>t</b> = time (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>Q = It</b> · <b>t = Q ÷ I</b></p>",1,""],["n-p18-for-e-m-f-and-p-d-from-work-do","phys-form",18,"E.m.f. and p.d. from work done","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">E = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">W</span><span class=\"de\">Q</span></span>   and   V = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">W</span><span class=\"de\">Q</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>E</b> = e.m.f. (V)</li><li><b>V</b> = p.d. (V)</li><li><b>W</b> = work done or energy transferred (J)</li><li><b>Q</b> = charge (C)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>W = VQ</b> · <b>Q = W ÷ V</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">1 V = 1 J/C.</p>",1,""],["n-p18-for-resistance","phys-form",18,"Resistance","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">R = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">V</span><span class=\"de\">I</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>R</b> = resistance (Ω)</li><li><b>V</b> = p.d. (V)</li><li><b>I</b> = current (A)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>V = IR</b> · <b>I = V ÷ R</b></p>",0,""],["n-p18-for-electrical-power","phys-form",18,"Electrical power","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">P = IV</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>P</b> = power (W)</li><li><b>I</b> = current (A)</li><li><b>V</b> = p.d. (V)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>I = P ÷ V</b> · <b>V = P ÷ I</b></p>",0,""],["n-p18-for-electrical-energy","phys-form",18,"Electrical energy","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">E = IVt</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>E</b> = energy (J)</li><li><b>I</b> = current (A)</li><li><b>V</b> = p.d. (V)</li><li><b>t</b> = time (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Also E = Pt. With P in kW and t in hours, E = Pt gives the energy in kW h.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-uni-current-units","phys-unit",18,"Current units","<p>1 mA = 0.001 A (10⁻³ A); 1 µA = 0.000 001 A (10⁻⁶ A). Current is measured in <strong>amperes (amps, A)</strong>; p.d. and e.m.f. in <strong>volts (V)</strong>; resistance in <strong>ohms (Ω)</strong>; charge in <strong>coulombs (C)</strong>; power in <strong>watts (W)</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-uni-energy-units","phys-unit",18,"Energy units","<p>1 kW h = 1 kW × 1 h = 1000 W × 3600 s = <strong>3 600 000 J</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-law-conservation-of-energy-in","phys-law",18,"Conservation of energy in a circuit","<p><strong>The energy given to the charges by the cell equals the total energy given by the charges to the components.</strong> So, in a series circuit, the <strong>e.m.f. = the sum of the p.d.s</strong> across the components.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-law-ohm-s-law-current-and-p-d","phys-law",18,"Ohm's law (current and p.d.)","<p><strong>For a metallic conductor at constant temperature, the current is directly proportional to the p.d.</strong> (resistance is constant). This is why the I–V graph for a resistor is a straight line through the origin.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-exp-determining-resistance-wit","phys-exp",18,"Determining resistance with a voltmeter and an ammeter","<p>Connect the resistor (or wire) in series with a cell, a switch, an <strong>ammeter</strong> and a <strong>variable resistor</strong>; connect a <strong>voltmeter in parallel</strong> across the resistor. Read V and I; change the variable resistor and take several pairs of readings. <strong>R = V ÷ I</strong> for each, then find the mean (or plot V against I: the gradient = R). Switch off between readings so the wire does not heat up.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-fac-electrons-flow-from-to-the","phys-facts",18,"Direction of electron flow compared with conventional current?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Electrons flow from − to +, the opposite way to conventional current.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p18-fac-despite-its-name-e-m-f-is","phys-facts",18,"Unit of e.m.f.? Is it a force?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Despite its name, e.m.f. is measured in volts; it is not a force.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-fac-voltmeters-are-always-conn","phys-facts",18,"How are voltmeters and ammeters connected?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Voltmeters are always connected in parallel; ammeters always in series.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-fac-for-the-same-p-d-a-bigger","phys-facts",18,"Same p.d., bigger resistance: what happens to the current?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">For the same p.d., a bigger resistance means a smaller current.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>I = V ÷ R.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p18-fac-a-resistor-works-the-same","phys-facts",18,"Resistor and diode: does the direction matter?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A resistor works the same either way round, but a diode does not.</p>",0,""],["n-p18-fac-a-longer-wire-has-more-res","phys-facts",18,"Effect of wire length on resistance?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A longer wire has more resistance.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> R ∝ length.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p18-fac-a-wire-with-a-larger-diame","phys-facts",18,"Effect of wire diameter (cross-sectional area) on resistance?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A wire with a larger diameter (cross-sectional area) has less resistance.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> R ∝ 1 ÷ area.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p18-fac-current-is-directly-propor","phys-facts",18,"Current and p.d. when the resistance is constant?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Current is directly proportional to p.d. when resistance is constant.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Ohm's law.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p18-fac-electric-circuits-transfer","phys-facts",18,"What do electric circuits transfer, from where and to where?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Electric circuits transfer energy from a source (a cell or the mains supply) to the circuit components, and then into the surroundings (e.g.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>as light and thermal energy).</p></div>",0,""],["n-p19-exp-advantages-of-lamps-in-par","phys-expl",19,"Advantages of lamps in parallel","<p>Each lamp gets the <strong>full supply p.d.</strong>, so it is fully bright; each can be <strong>switched on and off independently</strong>; if one lamp <strong>breaks</strong>, the others stay on.</p>",0,""],["n-p19-exp-mains-wiring","phys-expl",19,"Mains wiring","<p><strong>A mains circuit consists of a live (line) wire, a neutral wire and an earth wire.</strong> The <strong>switch must be in the live wire</strong>, so that when it is off, the appliance is disconnected from the high voltage. If it were in the neutral wire, the appliance would still be live when switched off.</p>",0,""],["n-p19-exp-hazards-of-mains-electrici","phys-expl",19,"Hazards of mains electricity","<p><strong>Damaged insulation:</strong> live wires can be touched → electric shock. <strong>Overheating cables:</strong> too much current → fire. <strong>Damp conditions:</strong> water conducts → shock. <strong>Overloading</strong> plugs, extension leads and sockets: too much current → overheating and fire.</p>",0,""],["n-p19-exp-building-series-and-parall","phys-expl",19,"Building series and parallel circuits","<p>Follow the circuit diagram one loop at a time. <strong>Ammeters</strong> go <strong>in series</strong> (break the circuit to insert them); <strong>voltmeters</strong> go <strong>in parallel</strong> across the component. Check the + and − terminals of meters and of diodes, and switch off between readings so components don't heat up.</p>",0,""],["n-p19-for-resistors-in-series","phys-form",19,"Resistors in series","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">R = R₁ + R₂ + R₃ + …</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>R</b> = combined resistance (Ω)</li></ul>",0,""],["n-p19-for-two-resistors-in-parallel","phys-form",19,"Two resistors in parallel","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">R</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">R₁</span></span> + <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">R₂</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>For two resistors only: R = (R₁ × R₂) ÷ (R₁ + R₂)</b></p><p class=\"qn-fn\">The combined resistance is less than the smaller resistor.</p>",1,""],["n-p19-for-potential-divider","phys-form",19,"Potential divider","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">R₁</span><span class=\"de\">R₂</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">V₁</span><span class=\"de\">V₂</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>R₁, R₂</b> = the two resistors in series (Ω)</li><li><b>V₁, V₂</b> = the p.d. across each (V)</li></ul>",1,""],["n-p19-for-series-and-parallel-rules","phys-form",19,"Series and parallel rules","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">Series: V = V₁ + V₂ + …   Parallel: I = I₁ + I₂ + …</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">Series: the current is the same everywhere. Parallel: the p.d. is the same across each branch.</p>",1,""],["n-p19-dif-series-circuits-vs-paralle","phys-diff",19,"Series circuits vs parallel circuits","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Series</th><th>Parallel</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Current</td><td><strong>Same</strong> at every point</td><td><strong>Splits</strong> between branches; total = sum of branch currents</td></tr>\n<tr><td>P.d.</td><td><strong>Shared</strong>; total = sum of the p.d.s</td><td><strong>Same</strong> across each branch</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Combined resistance</td><td><strong>Sum</strong> of resistances (bigger)</td><td><strong>Less</strong> than the smallest resistor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>If one lamp breaks</td><td>All go out</td><td>Others stay on</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p19-dif-fuse-vs-trip-switch","phys-diff",19,"Fuse vs trip switch","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fuse</th><th>Trip switch (circuit breaker)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>How it works</td><td>Wire <strong>melts</strong></td><td>Switch <strong>opens</strong> (electromagnet)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>After it acts</td><td>Must be <strong>replaced</strong></td><td>Can be <strong>reset</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slower</td><td>Faster</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p19-fac-in-parallel-the-p-d-is-the","phys-facts",19,"P.d. across each branch of a parallel circuit?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">In parallel, the p.d. is the same across each branch.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p19-fac-in-series-the-bigger-the-r","phys-facts",19,"In series, which resistor gets the bigger share of the p.d.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">In series, the bigger the resistance, the bigger its share of the p.d.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>For a constant current, V = IR rises as R rises.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p19-fac-the-current-at-every-point","phys-facts",19,"Current at different points in a series circuit?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The current at every point in a series circuit is the same.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The combined resistance is the <strong>sum</strong> of the resistances; cells in series add their e.m.f.s (if they face the same way).</p></div>",0,""],["n-p19-fac-the-current-from-the-sourc","phys-facts",19,"Parallel circuit: source current compared with branch currents? Combined resistance?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The current from the source is larger than the current in each branch. The combined resistance of two resistors in parallel is less than that of either resistor by itself.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Adding branches gives the current more paths.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p19-fac-the-sum-of-the-currents-en","phys-facts",19,"Currents entering and leaving a junction?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The sum of the currents entering a junction equals the sum of the currents leaving it.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>This is because charge is conserved: it is not created or destroyed at the junction.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p20-exp-magnetic-field-of-a-straig","phys-expl",20,"Magnetic field of a straight wire","<p><strong>Concentric circles</strong> around the wire. The direction is given by the <strong>right-hand grip rule</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> The field is <strong>strongest close to the wire</strong> and gets weaker further away; a <strong>bigger current</strong> gives a stronger field; <strong>reversing the current</strong> reverses the field direction.</p>",1,""],["n-p20-exp-magnetic-field-of-a-soleno","phys-expl",20,"Magnetic field of a solenoid","<p>Like the field of a <strong>bar magnet</strong>, with N and S poles at the ends; <strong>strong and uniform inside</strong> the coil. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> It is made stronger by <strong>more current</strong>, <strong>more turns</strong> and a <strong>soft-iron core</strong>; reversing the current swaps the poles.</p>",1,""],["n-p20-exp-relays-and-loudspeakers","phys-expl",20,"Relays and loudspeakers","<p><strong>Relay:</strong> current in a coil magnetises a soft-iron core, which attracts an iron armature and closes (or opens) contacts in a second circuit. <strong>Loudspeaker:</strong> an alternating current in a coil sitting in the field of a permanent magnet makes the coil, and the cone attached to it, <strong>vibrate</strong>, producing sound.</p>",0,""],["n-p20-exp-the-d-c-motor","phys-expl",20,"The d.c. motor","<p>Current flows through the coil; the two sides carry current in <strong>opposite directions</strong>, so the forces on them are in opposite directions (up and down): a turning effect. The <strong>split-ring commutator</strong> reverses the current in the coil <strong>every half turn</strong>, so the coil keeps turning in the same direction. <strong>Brushes</strong> (carbon) press on the commutator and connect it to the supply while letting it rotate.</p>",1,""],["n-p20-exp-force-on-beams-of-charged","phys-expl",20,"Force on beams of charged particles","<p>A beam of charged particles is a current, so it is deflected by a magnetic field. Use Fleming's left-hand rule with the <strong>conventional current</strong>: for electrons (negative), the current is <strong>opposite</strong> to their direction of motion.</p>",1,""],["n-p20-law-right-hand-grip-rule","phys-law",20,"Right-hand grip rule","<p><strong>Grip the wire with your right hand with the thumb pointing in the direction of the (conventional) current; your curled fingers show the direction of the magnetic field lines.</strong> For a solenoid: curl the fingers in the direction of the current in the turns; the thumb points to the N pole.</p>",0,""],["n-p20-law-fleming-s-left-hand-rule","phys-law",20,"Fleming's left-hand rule","<p><strong>Hold the thumb, first finger and second finger of the left hand at right angles: First finger = Field (N to S), seCond finger = Current (conventional), thuMb = Motion (force).</strong> It gives the direction of the force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field.</p>",1,""],["n-p20-exp-field-pattern-of-a-straigh","phys-exp",20,"Field pattern of a straight wire and a solenoid","<p>Pass the wire vertically through a horizontal card (or put the solenoid through it). Switch on a <strong>large current</strong>; sprinkle <strong>iron filings</strong> on the card and tap: they form circles (wire) or a bar-magnet pattern (solenoid). Place <strong>plotting compasses</strong> on the card to find the <strong>direction</strong>; reverse the current and the compasses reverse.</p>",0,""],["n-p20-exp-force-on-a-current-carryin","phys-exp",20,"Force on a current-carrying conductor","<p>Place a loose wire (or a light aluminium rod on two rails) between the poles of a <strong>horseshoe magnet</strong>, connected to a low-voltage supply. Switch on: the wire <strong>jumps</strong> (moves). <strong>Reverse the current:</strong> it moves the other way. <strong>Reverse the field</strong> (turn the magnet over): it moves the other way. Reverse both: same direction as at first.</p>",0,""],["n-p20-fac-the-turning-effect-of-a-mo","phys-facts",20,"Three ways to increase the turning effect of a motor?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The turning effect of a motor is increased by a stronger magnetic field, a larger current and more turns on the coil.</p>",0,""],["n-p20-fac-reversing-the-current-or-t","phys-facts",20,"Effect on the force of reversing the current, the field, or both?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Reversing the current or the field reverses the force; reversing both leaves it unchanged.</p>",0,""],["n-p20-fac-a-current-carrying-coil-in","phys-facts",20,"What may a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field experience?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A current-carrying coil in a magnetic field may experience a turning effect.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The turning effect increases with <strong>more turns</strong> on the coil, a <strong>larger current</strong>, and a <strong>stronger magnetic field</strong>.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p21-exp-factors-affecting-the-size","phys-expl",21,"Factors affecting the size of the induced e.m.f.","<p>A bigger e.m.f. is induced by moving the magnet (or wire) <strong>faster</strong>, using a <strong>stronger magnet</strong>, and using a coil with <strong>more turns</strong>. If the magnet is <strong>stationary</strong>, there is <strong>no</strong> induced e.m.f.</p>",0,""],["n-p21-exp-the-a-c-generator","phys-expl",21,"The a.c. generator","<p>A coil is <strong>rotated</strong> in a magnetic field (or a magnet is rotated inside a coil). The sides of the coil cut the field lines, inducing an e.m.f. that <strong>reverses every half turn</strong>: alternating current. <strong>Slip rings</strong> (one for each end of the coil) and <strong>brushes</strong> connect the rotating coil to the external circuit without the wires twisting.</p>",1,""],["n-p21-exp-generator-output-and-coil","phys-expl",21,"Generator output and coil position","<p>The e.m.f. is <strong>maximum (peak or trough)</strong> when the plane of the coil is <strong>parallel</strong> to the field lines (the sides cut the field lines fastest). It is <strong>zero</strong> when the coil is <strong>perpendicular</strong> to the field lines (the sides move along the field lines). Spinning faster makes the peaks <strong>higher</strong> and <strong>closer together</strong> (higher frequency).</p>",1,""],["n-p21-exp-how-a-transformer-works","phys-expl",21,"How a transformer works","<p>The <strong>alternating current</strong> in the primary coil produces a <strong>changing (alternating) magnetic field</strong> in the <strong>soft-iron core</strong>. The core carries this changing field through the secondary coil, which <strong>induces an alternating e.m.f.</strong> in the secondary. A transformer does not work with d.c., because the field would not change.</p>",1,""],["n-p21-exp-high-voltage-transmission","phys-expl",21,"High-voltage transmission","<p>Power stations use <strong>step-up</strong> transformers to transmit electricity at a <strong>very high voltage</strong>; <strong>step-down</strong> transformers reduce it for homes. For the same power, a high voltage means a <strong>small current</strong>, so <strong>less energy is lost as thermal energy in the cables</strong> (P = I²R), and <strong>thinner, cheaper cables</strong> can be used.</p>",1,""],["n-p21-for-transformer-equation","phys-form",21,"Transformer equation","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Vₚ</span><span class=\"de\">Vₛ</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">Nₚ</span><span class=\"de\">Nₛ</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>V</b> = voltage (V)</li><li><b>N</b> = number of turns</li><li><b>p</b> = primary</li><li><b>s</b> = secondary</li></ul><p class=\"qn-re\"><span class=\"qn-lab\">Rearranged</span> <b>Vₛ = Vₚ × Nₛ ÷ Nₚ</b></p>",0,""],["n-p21-for-100-efficient-transformer","phys-form",21,"100% efficient transformer","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">IₚVₚ = IₛVₛ</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>I</b> = current (A)</li><li><b>V</b> = voltage (V)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Power in = power out. Stepping the voltage up steps the current down by the same factor.</p>",1,""],["n-p21-for-power-lost-in-cables","phys-form",21,"Power lost in cables","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">P = I²R</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>P</b> = power lost as thermal energy (W)</li><li><b>I</b> = current in the cable (A)</li><li><b>R</b> = resistance of the cable (Ω)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Halving the current makes the loss four times smaller.</p>",1,""],["n-p21-law-fleming-s-right-hand-rule","phys-law",21,"Fleming's right-hand rule","<p><strong>Hold the thumb, first finger and second finger of the right hand at right angles: thuMb = Motion of the wire, First finger = Field (N to S), seCond finger = induced Current.</strong> It gives the direction of the induced current in a wire moving across a magnetic field.</p>",1,""],["n-p21-law-direction-of-an-induced-e","phys-law",21,"Direction of an induced e.m.f. (Lenz's law)","<p><strong>The direction of an induced e.m.f. opposes the change causing it.</strong> e.g. pushing the N pole of a magnet into a coil makes that end of the coil a N pole, which repels the magnet. Pulling the magnet out, or using the other pole, <strong>reverses</strong> the e.m.f.</p>",1,""],["n-p21-dif-slip-rings-vs-split-ring-c","phys-diff",21,"Slip rings vs split-ring commutator","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Slip rings</th><th>Split-ring commutator</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Used in</td><td><strong>a.c. generator</strong></td><td><strong>d.c. motor</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shape</td><td>Two complete rings, one for each end of the coil</td><td>One ring split into two halves</td></tr>\n<tr><td>What it does</td><td>Keeps each end of the coil connected to the same brush, so the output is <strong>alternating</strong></td><td>Reverses the current in the coil <strong>every half turn</strong>, so the motor keeps turning the same way</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-p21-dif-step-up-vs-step-down-trans","phys-diff",21,"Step-up vs step-down transformer","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Step-up</th><th>Step-down</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Turns</td><td>Nₛ <strong>more</strong> than Nₚ</td><td>Nₛ <strong>fewer</strong> than Nₚ</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Output voltage</td><td><strong>Higher</strong> than input</td><td><strong>Lower</strong> than input</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Output current (100% efficient)</td><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use</td><td>At power stations, before transmission</td><td>Near homes and in chargers</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p21-exp-demonstrating-electromagne","phys-exp",21,"Demonstrating electromagnetic induction","<p>Connect a coil to a <strong>sensitive centre-zero ammeter</strong> (galvanometer). Push a bar magnet <strong>into</strong> the coil: the needle flicks one way. <strong>Hold it still:</strong> no reading. Pull it <strong>out</strong>: the needle flicks the <strong>other</strong> way. Move it <strong>faster</strong>: a bigger reading. (Or move a straight wire up and down between the poles of a horseshoe magnet.)</p>",0,""],["n-p21-fac-an-induced-e-m-f-needs-rel","phys-facts",21,"What is needed to induce an e.m.f.?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">An induced e.m.f. needs relative movement (or a changing field): a stationary magnet in a coil induces nothing.</p>",0,""],["n-p21-fac-high-voltage-is-used-for-t","phys-facts",21,"Why is high voltage used for transmission?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">High voltage is used for transmission to reduce the energy lost by heating in the cables.</p>",0,""],["n-p21-fac-transformers-only-work-wit","phys-facts",21,"Which type of current do transformers work with?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Transformers only work with alternating current.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p22-exp-structure-of-the-atom","phys-expl",22,"Structure of the atom","<p><strong>An atom has a small, positively charged nucleus, with negatively charged electrons in orbit around it.</strong> The nucleus contains <strong>protons</strong> and <strong>neutrons</strong>. An atom is neutral: number of electrons = number of protons.</p>",0,""],["n-p22-exp-nuclide-notation","phys-expl",22,"Nuclide notation","<p>Written as <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>A</span><span>Z</span></span>X</span>, with the <strong>nucleon number A on top</strong> and the <strong>proton number Z below</strong>, e.g. <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>14</span><span>6</span></span>C</span> has 6 protons and 14 − 6 = <strong>8 neutrons</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p22-exp-alpha-particle-scattering","phys-expl",22,"Alpha-particle scattering","<p>α-particles fired at <strong>thin gold foil</strong>: (a) <strong>most went straight through</strong> → the atom is mostly <strong>empty space</strong> with a <strong>very small nucleus</strong>; (b) <strong>a few were deflected through large angles</strong> (some bounced back) → the nucleus contains <strong>most of the mass</strong> of the atom; (c) they were <strong>repelled</strong> → the nucleus is <strong>positively charged</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-p22-exp-mass-and-energy-in-fission","phys-expl",22,"Mass and energy in fission and fusion","<p><strong>Fission</strong> (a heavy nucleus splits) and <strong>fusion</strong> (light nuclei join) both <strong>release energy</strong>. In both, the total <strong>mass of the products is slightly less</strong> than the mass before; this mass has been converted into energy (no values needed). Fission: uranium-235 absorbs a neutron and splits into two smaller nuclei plus more neutrons, e.g. <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>235</span><span>92</span></span>U</span> + <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>1</span><span>0</span></span>n</span> → <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>141</span><span>56</span></span>Ba</span> + <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>92</span><span>36</span></span>Kr</span> + 3<span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>1</span><span>0</span></span>n</span>. Fusion: hydrogen nuclei join to form helium in the Sun. Definitions: Ch 7, items 9 and 14.</p>",1,""],["n-p22-for-number-of-neutrons","phys-form",22,"Number of neutrons","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">number of neutrons = A − Z</div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>A</b> = nucleon (mass) number</li><li><b>Z</b> = proton (atomic) number</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">From: nucleon number = proton number + number of neutrons.</p>",0,""],["n-p22-dif-proton-vs-neutron-vs-elect","phys-diff",22,"Proton vs neutron vs electron","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Particle</th><th>Where</th><th>Relative charge</th><th>Relative mass</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Proton</td><td>Nucleus</td><td><strong>+1</strong></td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Neutron</td><td>Nucleus</td><td><strong>0</strong></td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Electron</td><td>Orbits the nucleus</td><td><strong>−1</strong></td><td>about 1/1840 (negligible)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p22-dif-nuclear-fission-vs-nuclear","phys-diff",22,"Nuclear fission vs nuclear fusion","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Fission</th><th>Fusion</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What happens</td><td>A <strong>heavy</strong> nucleus <strong>splits</strong> into two smaller nuclei</td><td>Two <strong>light</strong> nuclei <strong>join</strong> to form a heavier nucleus</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>Uranium-235 in nuclear power stations</td><td>Hydrogen → helium in the Sun and stars</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Conditions</td><td>Nucleus absorbs a neutron</td><td>Very high temperature and pressure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Energy</td><td>Released (mass of products slightly less)</td><td>Released (mass of products slightly less)</td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-p22-fac-the-nucleus-is-tiny-compar","phys-facts",22,"Size and mass of the nucleus compared with the whole atom?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The nucleus is tiny compared with the whole atom but contains almost all of its mass.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p22-fac-isotopes-of-an-element-hav","phys-facts",22,"Why do isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Isotopes of an element have the same chemical properties, because they have the same number of electrons.</p>",0,""],["n-p22-fac-atoms-form-positive-ions-b","phys-facts",22,"How do atoms form positive and negative ions?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Atoms form positive ions by losing electrons, or negative ions by gaining electrons.</p>",0,""],["n-p22-fac-the-relative-charge-on-a-n","phys-facts",22,"Relative charge on a nucleus?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The <strong>relative charge</strong> on a nucleus is equal to its <strong>proton number Z</strong>.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The <strong>relative mass</strong> of a nucleus is approximately equal to its <strong>nucleon number A</strong>.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p23-exp-gamma-emission","phys-expl",23,"Gamma emission","<p>A γ-ray is emitted when the nucleus loses extra energy; <strong>A and Z do not change</strong>, so the element stays the same. All three types of decay make the nucleus <strong>more stable</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-p23-exp-relative-ionising-effects","phys-expl",23,"Relative ionising effects","<p>α-particles are the <strong>most ionising</strong> because they have a <strong>large charge (+2)</strong> and <strong>high kinetic energy</strong> (heavy, so they hit atoms hard). β-particles are less ionising (charge −1, smaller mass). γ-rays are the <strong>least ionising</strong> because they have <strong>no charge</strong>.</p>",1,""],["n-p23-exp-choosing-an-isotope","phys-expl",23,"Choosing an isotope","<p>The <strong>type of radiation</strong> and the <strong>half-life</strong> decide the use: <strong>(a) Smoke alarms:</strong> α-emitter (ionises the air, absorbed by smoke; long half-life so it lasts for years). <strong>(b) Irradiating food</strong> and <strong>(c) sterilising equipment:</strong> γ (penetrates packaging and kills bacteria). <strong>(d) Thickness control:</strong> β for paper (partly absorbed, so the count rate changes with thickness), γ for thick metal sheets; long half-life so the count rate stays steady. <strong>(e) Diagnosis and treatment of cancer:</strong> γ (penetrates the body); short half-life for tracers inside the body.</p>",1,""],["n-p23-exp-safe-handling","phys-expl",23,"Safe handling","<p>Move sources with <strong>long tongs</strong> and point them away from people; store them in <strong>lead-lined boxes</strong> and keep them locked away; wear protective clothing and a badge to monitor exposure. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> All precautions <strong>reduce exposure time</strong>, <strong>increase the distance</strong> between source and living tissue, and use <strong>shielding</strong> to absorb radiation.</p>",1,""],["n-p23-for-corrected-count-rate","phys-form",23,"Corrected count rate","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">corrected count rate = measured count rate − background count rate</div>",1,""],["n-p23-for-fraction-left-after-n-half","phys-form",23,"Fraction left after n half-lives","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">fraction remaining = (½)ⁿ</div><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. after 3 half-lives, ⅛ of the nuclei remain. Not in the syllabus list, but it makes half-life questions quicker.</p>",0,""],["n-p23-dif-alpha-vs-beta-vs-gamma","phys-diff",23,"Alpha vs beta vs gamma","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Alpha (α)</th><th>Beta (β)</th><th>Gamma (γ)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Nature</td><td><strong>Helium nucleus</strong> (2 protons + 2 neutrons)</td><td><strong>Fast electron</strong> from the nucleus</td><td><strong>Electromagnetic radiation</strong> (wave)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Charge</td><td><strong>+2</strong></td><td><strong>−1</strong></td><td><strong>0</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Relative mass</td><td>4</td><td>about 1/1840</td><td>0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ionising effect</td><td><strong>Strongest</strong></td><td>Medium</td><td><strong>Weakest</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Penetration (stopped by)</td><td><strong>Paper</strong> or a few cm of air (cannot get through skin)</td><td>A few mm of <strong>aluminium</strong></td><td>Reduced by thick <strong>lead</strong> or concrete</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deflection in electric and magnetic fields <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></td><td>Small deflection</td><td>Large deflection, opposite direction to α</td><td><strong>Not deflected</strong></td></tr>\n</table>",1,""],["n-p23-fac-effects-of-ionising-radiat","phys-facts",23,"Effects of ionising radiation on living things?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Effects of ionising radiation on living things: cell death, mutations and cancer.</p>",0,""],["n-p23-fac-uses-of-radioisotopes-smok","phys-facts",23,"Uses of radioisotopes, and the radiation each uses?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Uses of radioisotopes: smoke alarms (α), irradiating food (γ), sterilising equipment (γ), thickness control (β or γ), diagnosis and treatment of cancer (γ).<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>See \"Choosing an isotope\" above.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p23-fac-alpha-particles-cannot-pen","phys-facts",23,"Can alpha particles penetrate skin?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alpha particles cannot penetrate skin.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>They are dangerous only if the source is swallowed or breathed in.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p23-fac-alpha-particles-are-the-mo","phys-facts",23,"Alpha particles: penetrating power and ionising effect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Alpha particles are the most easily absorbed (least penetrating) and the most ionising.</p>",0,""],["n-p23-fac-gamma-radiation-is-the-mos","phys-facts",23,"Gamma radiation: penetrating power and ionising effect?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Gamma radiation is the most penetrating and the least ionising.</p>",0,""],["n-p24-exp-day-and-night","phys-expl",24,"Day and night","<p>The Earth <strong>rotates on its tilted axis</strong> once in about <strong>24 hours</strong>. This causes the periodic cycle of <strong>day and night</strong> and the <strong>apparent daily motion of the Sun</strong> (rising in the east, setting in the west).</p>",0,""],["n-p24-exp-seasons","phys-expl",24,"Seasons","<p>The Earth <strong>orbits the Sun</strong> once in about <strong>365 days</strong>. Because its axis is <strong>tilted</strong>, each hemisphere leans <strong>towards</strong> the Sun for part of the year (summer: Sun higher in the sky, longer days) and <strong>away</strong> for the other part (winter).</p>",0,""],["n-p24-exp-phases-of-the-moon","phys-expl",24,"Phases of the Moon","<p>The Moon takes about <strong>one month</strong> to orbit the Earth. It does not give out light; we see the part lit by the Sun. As it orbits, we see different amounts of its lit half, giving the cycle of <strong>phases</strong> (new moon → full moon → new moon).</p>",0,""],["n-p24-exp-the-solar-system","phys-expl",24,"The Solar System","<p>It contains <strong>one star (the Sun)</strong>; the <strong>eight planets</strong>; <strong>minor planets</strong> that orbit the Sun, including <strong>dwarf planets</strong> (e.g. Pluto) and <strong>asteroids</strong> in the asteroid belt; <strong>moons</strong> that orbit the planets; and smaller bodies including <strong>comets</strong> and natural satellites.</p>",0,""],["n-p24-exp-formation-of-the-solar-sys","phys-expl",24,"Formation of the Solar System (accretion model)","<p>The Solar System formed from an <strong>interstellar cloud of gas and dust</strong> containing many elements. <strong>Gravity</strong> pulled the cloud together; it <strong>rotated</strong> and flattened into an <strong>accretion disc</strong>. Near the hot young Sun, only rocky material could stay solid, forming the <strong>four small, rocky inner planets</strong>. Further out it was cold enough for gases and ices to collect, forming the <strong>four large, gaseous outer planets</strong>.</p>",0,""],["n-p24-exp-speeds-in-orbit","phys-expl",24,"Speeds in orbit","<p>The Sun's gravitational field gets <strong>weaker further from the Sun</strong>, so planets further out have <strong>lower orbital speeds</strong> (and longer orbits). An object in an elliptical orbit travels <strong>faster when it is closer to the Sun</strong>: as it falls inwards it loses <strong>gravitational potential energy</strong> and gains <strong>kinetic energy</strong> (conservation of energy); moving away, it slows down.</p>",1,""],["n-p24-for-average-orbital-speed","phys-form",24,"Average orbital speed","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">v = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">2πr</span><span class=\"de\">T</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>v</b> = average orbital speed (m/s or km/s)</li><li><b>r</b> = average radius of the orbit (m or km)</li><li><b>T</b> = orbital period (s)</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">Keep units consistent: km with km/s.</p>",1,""],["n-p24-for-time-for-light-to-travel","phys-form",24,"Time for light to travel","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">time = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">distance</span><span class=\"de\">speed of light</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>speed of light</b> = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s</li></ul><p class=\"qn-fn\">e.g. Sun to Earth: 1.5 × 10¹¹ m ÷ 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s = 500 s (about 8 minutes).</p>",0,""],["n-p24-dif-asteroids-vs-comets","phys-diff",24,"Asteroids vs comets","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Asteroid</th><th>Comet</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Made of</td><td><strong>Rock</strong> (and metal)</td><td><strong>Ice, dust and gas</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Orbit</td><td>Mostly nearly circular, in the <strong>asteroid belt</strong> between Mars and Jupiter</td><td><strong>Very elongated ellipse</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tail?</td><td>No</td><td>Grows a <strong>tail</strong> when close to the Sun (ice evaporates)</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p24-dif-inner-planets-vs-outer-pla","phys-diff",24,"Inner planets vs outer planets","<table class=\"qn-t\">\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Four inner planets</th><th>Four outer planets</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Planets</td><td>Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars</td><td>Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Type</td><td><strong>Rocky</strong></td><td><strong>Gaseous</strong> (gas giants)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Size</td><td><strong>Small</strong></td><td><strong>Large</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Why</td><td>Near the hot Sun, only rocky material stayed solid</td><td>Further out, cold enough for gases and ices to collect</td></tr>\n</table>",0,""],["n-p24-fac-order-of-the-planets-from","phys-facts",24,"Order of the planets from the Sun?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Order of the planets from the Sun: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Mnemonic: <strong>M</strong>y <strong>V</strong>ery <strong>E</strong>asy <strong>M</strong>ethod <strong>J</strong>ust <strong>S</strong>peeds <strong>U</strong>p <strong>N</strong>aming.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p24-fac-pluto-and-eris-are-dwarf-p","phys-facts",24,"Pluto and Eris are…?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Pluto and Eris are dwarf planets (minor planets).</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>There are only <strong>eight planets</strong>; there are many thousands of minor planets (dwarf planets and asteroids).</p></div>",0,""],["n-p24-fac-phases-of-the-moon-in-orde","phys-facts",24,"Phases of the Moon, in order?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Phases of the Moon, in order: new moon, waxing crescent, first quarter, waxing gibbous, full moon, waning gibbous, third (last) quarter, waning crescent.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The names are not required; you must be able to explain why phases happen.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p24-fac-the-gravitational-field-st","phys-facts",24,"What does the gravitational field strength at a planet&#x27;s surface depend on?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The gravitational field strength <strong>at the surface</strong> of a planet depends on the <strong>mass</strong> of the planet.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Around a planet, it <strong>decreases as the distance from the planet increases</strong>.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p24-fac-the-force-that-keeps-an-ob","phys-facts",24,"What force keeps an object in orbit around the Sun?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The force that keeps an object in orbit around the Sun is the gravitational attraction of the Sun.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The Sun contains <strong>most of the mass</strong> of the Solar System, which is why the planets orbit it.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p25-exp-measuring-galaxies","phys-expl",25,"Measuring galaxies","<p>The <strong>speed v</strong> at which a galaxy is moving away is found from the <strong>change in wavelength</strong> of its starlight (redshift). The <strong>distance d</strong> of a far galaxy can be found from the <strong>brightness of a supernova</strong> in that galaxy.</p>",1,""],["n-p25-exp-life-cycle-of-a-star","phys-expl",25,"Life cycle of a star","<p>(a) Interstellar cloud of gas and dust (hydrogen) → (b) <strong>protostar</strong> → (c) <strong>stable star</strong> (forces balanced) → (d) the star runs out of hydrogen in its centre → (e) it expands: <strong>most stars</strong> form a <strong>red giant</strong>; <strong>more massive stars</strong> form a <strong>red supergiant</strong>. (f) A red giant (less massive star) forms a <strong>planetary nebula</strong> with a <strong>white dwarf</strong> at its centre. (g) A red supergiant explodes as a <strong>supernova</strong>, leaving a <strong>neutron star</strong> or a <strong>black hole</strong>. (h) The nebula from a supernova may form new stars with planets.</p>",1,""],["n-p25-for-hubble-constant","phys-form",25,"Hubble constant","<div class=\"qn-fbox\">H₀ = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">v</span><span class=\"de\">d</span></span></div><ul class=\"qn-sym\"><li><b>H₀</b> = Hubble constant (s⁻¹)</li><li><b>v</b> = speed at which the galaxy moves away (m/s)</li><li><b>d</b> = distance of the galaxy from Earth (m)</li></ul>",1,""],["n-p25-for-age-of-the-universe","phys-form",25,"Age of the Universe","<div class=\"qn-fbox\"><span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">d</span><span class=\"de\">v</span></span> = <span class=\"frac\"><span class=\"nu\">1</span><span class=\"de\">H₀</span></span></div><p class=\"qn-fn\">This is an estimate of the age of the Universe (the time since all its matter was at a single point). Your item 51 says the same: age = reciprocal of the Hubble constant.</p>",1,""],["n-p25-uni-astronomical-values","phys-unit",25,"Astronomical values","<p>1 light-year = 9.5 × 10¹⁵ m. Diameter of the Milky Way ≈ 100 000 light-years. H₀ ≈ 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ s⁻¹. Speed of light = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.</p>",1,""],["n-p25-law-big-bang-theory","phys-law",25,"Big Bang theory","<p><strong>The Universe began from a single point (all its matter together) and has been expanding ever since.</strong> Evidence: redshift of distant galaxies (the Universe is expanding) and <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> the CMBR.</p>",0,""],["n-p25-law-hubble-s-law","phys-law",25,"Hubble's law","<p><strong>The speed at which a galaxy is moving away from the Earth is proportional to its distance from the Earth (v = H₀d).</strong></p>",1,""],["n-p25-fac-the-current-estimate-for-h","phys-facts",25,"Current estimate of H₀?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The current estimate for H₀ is 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ per second.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p25-fac-age-of-the-universe-1-h-4","phys-facts",25,"Age of the Universe, in seconds and in years?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Age of the Universe ≈ 1 ÷ H₀ ≈ 4.5 × 10¹⁷ s ≈ 14 billion years (14.4 × 10⁹ years).<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p>",1,""],["n-p25-fac-the-milky-way-is-one-of-ma","phys-facts",25,"The Milky Way: how many stars, and how big?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The Milky Way is one of many billions of galaxies; it contains many billions of stars and is about 100 000 light-years across.</p>",0,""],["n-p25-fac-the-sun-radiates-most-of-i","phys-facts",25,"In which regions does the Sun radiate most of its energy?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The Sun radiates most of its energy as infrared, visible light and ultraviolet.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>Gamma rays are made by fusion deep inside, but the Sun does <strong>not</strong> emit much of its energy as gamma rays.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p25-fac-the-sun-is-a-star-of-mediu","phys-facts",25,"The Sun: size and composition?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">The Sun is a star of medium size, consisting mostly of hydrogen and helium.<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span></p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Stars are powered by <strong>nuclear reactions</strong> that release energy; in stable stars, <strong>hydrogen nuclei fuse into helium</strong>.</p></div>",1,""],["n-p25-fac-a-galaxy-is-made-up-of-man","phys-facts",25,"What is a galaxy made of?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">A galaxy is made up of many billions of stars.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The Sun is a star in the galaxy called the <strong>Milky Way</strong>. The other stars in the Milky Way are much further from the Earth than the Sun is.</p></div>",0,""],["n-p25-fac-redshift-in-the-light-from","phys-facts",25,"What does the redshift of distant galaxies show?","<p class=\"qn-lead\">Redshift in the light from distant galaxies is evidence that the Universe is expanding and supports the Big Bang theory.</p><div class=\"qn-more\"><p>The further away a galaxy is, the greater its redshift, so the faster it is moving away.</p></div>",0,""]],"defs":[["d-p1-density",1,"Density","<p>Density is mass per unit volume.</p>","<p>It tells you how tightly packed the mass is. Unit <strong>kg/m³</strong> or <strong>g/cm³</strong>. Water has a density of 1.0 g/cm³ (1000 kg/m³).</p>",0],["d-p2-speed",2,"Speed","<p>Speed is the distance travelled per unit time.</p>","<p>Unit m/s (also km/h, cm/s).</p>",0],["d-p2-velocity",2,"Velocity","<p>Velocity is speed in a given direction.</p>","<p>It is a vector; speed is a scalar.</p>",0],["d-p2-acceleration",2,"Acceleration<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Acceleration is the change in velocity per unit time.</p>","<p>Unit m/s². <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> A <strong>deceleration</strong> is a <strong>negative acceleration</strong>.</p>",1],["d-p2-scalar-and-vector-quantities",2,"Scalar and vector quantities<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A scalar quantity has magnitude (size) only.</p><p>A vector quantity has magnitude and direction.</p>","<p><strong>Scalars:</strong> distance, speed, time, mass, energy, temperature. <strong>Vectors:</strong> force, weight, velocity, acceleration, momentum, electric field strength, gravitational field strength.</p>",1],["d-p3-mass",3,"Mass","<p>Mass is a measure of the quantity of matter in an object at rest relative to the observer.</p>","<p>Unit kg. Mass does not change from place to place.</p>",0],["d-p3-weight",3,"Weight<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Weight is a gravitational force on an object that has mass.</p>","<p>Unit N. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> Weight is <strong>the effect of a gravitational field on a mass</strong>, so it changes if g changes (e.g. on the Moon).</p>",1],["d-p3-gravitational-field-strength",3,"Gravitational field strength","<p>Gravitational field strength is the force per unit mass.</p>","<p>g = W ÷ m. Unit N/kg. It is <strong>equivalent to the acceleration of free fall</strong> (9.8 N/kg = 9.8 m/s² on Earth).</p>",0],["d-p3-momentum",3,"Momentum<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Momentum is mass × velocity.</p>","<p>p = mv. Unit kg m/s. A vector.</p>",1],["d-p3-impulse",3,"Impulse<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Impulse is force × time for which the force acts.</p>","<p>Impulse = FΔt = Δ(mv), the change in momentum. Unit N s.</p>",1],["d-p3-resultant-force",3,"Resultant force<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Resultant force is the change in momentum per unit time.</p>","<p>It is also the single force that has the same effect as all the forces acting on an object. For forces along one line, add those in the same direction and subtract those in opposite directions.</p>",1],["d-p3-friction-and-drag",3,"Friction and drag","<p>Solid friction is the force between two surfaces that may impede motion and produce heating.</p>","<p><strong>Drag</strong> is friction on an object moving through a liquid or a gas (<strong>air resistance</strong>).</p>",0],["d-p4-moment-of-a-force",4,"Moment of a force","<p>The moment of a force is a measure of its turning effect.</p><p>Moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot.</p>","<p>Unit <strong>N m</strong>. Everyday examples: pushing a door far from its hinges, a spanner, a see-saw, a wheelbarrow.</p>",0],["d-p4-centre-of-gravity",4,"Centre of gravity","<p>The point through which the whole weight of an object can be considered to act.</p>","<p>For a uniform, symmetrical object it is at the centre. If the object is hung from a point, it settles with its centre of gravity directly below that point.</p>",0],["d-p4-equilibrium",4,"Equilibrium","<p>When there is no resultant force and no resultant moment, an object is in equilibrium.</p>","",0],["d-p5-limit-of-proportionality",5,"Limit of proportionality<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The point on a load–extension graph beyond which the extension is no longer proportional to the load.</p>","<p>Up to this point the graph is a <strong>straight line through the origin</strong>; after it, the line curves. (Elastic limit is not required.)</p>",1],["d-p5-spring-constant",5,"Spring constant<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The spring constant is the force per unit extension.</p>","<p>k = F ÷ x. Unit N/m (or N/cm). A stiffer spring has a larger k.</p>",1],["d-p5-pressure",5,"Pressure","<p>Pressure is the force per unit area.</p>","<p>p = F ÷ A. Unit <strong>Pa</strong> (= N/m²); N/cm² is also used.</p>",0],["d-p6-sankey-diagram",6,"Sankey diagram<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A flow diagram showing energy transfers, in which the width of each arrow is proportional to the amount of energy.</p>","<p>The input arrow splits into a useful output arrow and a wasted output arrow; input width = useful + wasted (conservation of energy).</p>",1],["d-p6-efficiency",6,"Efficiency<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The fraction (or percentage) of the energy supplied that is usefully transferred.</p>","<p>No device is 100% efficient: some energy is always wasted, usually as thermal energy (heating) and sound.</p>",1],["d-p7-renewable-and-non-renewable-re",7,"Renewable and non-renewable resources","<p>A renewable resource is replaced naturally and will not run out.</p><p>A non-renewable resource cannot be replaced once used up.</p>","<p><strong>Renewable:</strong> biofuels, hydroelectric, tides, waves, wind, geothermal, solar. <strong>Non-renewable:</strong> fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas) and nuclear fuel (uranium).</p>",0],["d-p7-photovoltaic-cell-solar-cell",7,"Photovoltaic cell (solar cell)","<p>A device that transfers the energy of light from the Sun directly into electrical energy.</p>","<p>No moving parts and no fuel, but it only works in daylight and each cell gives a small output.</p>",0],["d-p7-solar-panel",7,"Solar panel","<p>A panel that uses infrared (and other electromagnetic waves) from the Sun to heat water.</p>","<p>Different from a solar cell, which makes electricity.</p>",0],["d-p7-biofuel",7,"Biofuel","<p>A fuel made from plant or animal material, which stores chemical energy.</p>","<p>e.g. <strong>wood, animal dung, ethanol from sugar cane, biogas</strong> from rotting waste. Renewable, because new plants can be grown.</p>",0],["d-p7-nuclear-fission",7,"Nuclear fission<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The splitting of a large, heavy nucleus into two smaller nuclei, releasing energy.</p>","<p>Used in nuclear power stations (fuel: uranium). The energy heats water in a boiler.</p>",1],["d-p7-geothermal-energy",7,"Geothermal energy","<p>Energy stored in hot rocks underground.</p>","<p>Cold water is pumped down, comes back up as steam and drives a turbine.</p>",0],["d-p7-tidal-energy",7,"Tidal energy","<p>Energy from the movement of the tides; water flowing in and out through a barrage turns turbines.</p>","<p>Tides are caused mainly by the gravity of the <strong>Moon</strong>. Reliable and predictable (two tides a day); produces no CO₂ while generating, but barrages damage estuary habitats.</p>",0],["d-p7-nuclear-fusion",7,"Nuclear fusion<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The joining of two light nuclei (e.g. hydrogen) to form a heavier nucleus (e.g. helium), releasing energy.</p>","<p>It needs a very <strong>high temperature and pressure</strong>, so that the nuclei collide fast enough to fuse. <strong>Energy is released by nuclear fusion in the Sun.</strong></p>",1],["d-p8-work-done",8,"Work done","<p>Mechanical or electrical work done is equal to the energy transferred.</p><p>Mechanical work done = force × distance moved in the direction of the force.</p>","<p>Unit <strong>joule (J)</strong>. 1 J is the work done when a force of 1 N moves through 1 m.</p>",0],["d-p8-power",8,"Power","<p>Power is the work done per unit time, and also the energy transferred per unit time.</p>","<p>Unit <strong>watt (W)</strong>; 1 W = 1 J/s.</p>",0],["d-p9-absolute-zero",9,"Absolute zero","<p>The lowest possible temperature, −273 °C (0 K), at which the particles have the least kinetic energy.</p>","<p>Nothing can be cooled below it.</p>",0],["d-p9-brownian-motion",9,"Brownian motion<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The random motion of microscopic particles in a suspension, caused by random collisions with the particles of the gas or liquid.</p>","<p>e.g. smoke particles in air, seen with a microscope, jiggle about randomly. It is <strong>evidence for the kinetic particle model</strong>: the air molecules are too small to see but are moving fast in all directions. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> The <strong>microscopic particles</strong> (smoke, pollen) are moved by collisions with <strong>light, fast-moving molecules</strong>; use \"atoms\" or \"molecules\" for the air and \"microscopic particles\" for the smoke.</p>",1],["d-p9-temperature",9,"Temperature<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A measure of how hot or cold an object is.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> A rise in temperature is an <strong>increase in the average kinetic energy</strong> of the particles. Measured in °C or K.</p>",1],["d-p10-thermal-expansion",10,"Thermal expansion","<p>The increase in volume of a material when its temperature rises (at constant pressure).</p>","<p>Solids, liquids and gases all expand when heated; gases expand the most.</p>",0],["d-p10-specific-heat-capacity",10,"Specific heat capacity<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The energy required per unit mass per unit temperature increase.</p>","<p>In other words, the energy needed to raise the temperature of <strong>1 kg</strong> of the substance by <strong>1 °C</strong>. Unit <strong>J/(kg °C)</strong> (or J/(g °C)). Water has a high value (4200 J/(kg °C)), so it heats up and cools down slowly.</p>",1],["d-p10-evaporation",10,"Evaporation","<p>Evaporation is the escape of the more energetic particles from the surface of a liquid.</p>","<p>It happens at any temperature.</p>",0],["d-p11-thermal-conduction",11,"Thermal conduction","<p>The transfer of thermal energy through a material without the material itself moving.</p>","<p>Good thermal conductors (metals) transfer it quickly; bad conductors are <strong>thermal insulators</strong> (plastic, wood, air).</p>",0],["d-p11-convection",11,"Convection","<p>The transfer of thermal energy through a liquid or a gas by the movement of the fluid itself.</p>","<p>Convection is an important method of thermal energy transfer in liquids and gases. It cannot happen in solids.</p>",0],["d-p11-convection-current",11,"Convection current","<p>The circulation of a liquid or gas caused by warmer, less dense fluid rising and cooler, denser fluid sinking.</p>","<p>When part of a fluid is heated it <strong>expands</strong>, becomes <strong>less dense</strong> and <strong>rises</strong>; cooler, <strong>denser</strong> fluid <strong>sinks</strong> to take its place.</p>",0],["d-p11-thermal-radiation",11,"Thermal radiation","<p>Thermal radiation is infrared radiation; all objects emit it.</p>","<p>It is an electromagnetic wave, so it <strong>does not need a medium</strong> and can travel through empty space (a vacuum).</p>",0],["d-p12-compression",12,"Compression<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A region of a sound wave where the particles are pushed close together (higher pressure).</p>","",1],["d-p12-rarefaction",12,"Rarefaction<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A region of a sound wave where the particles are further apart (lower pressure).</p>","",1],["d-p12-amplitude",12,"Amplitude","<p>The greatest height or depth of a wave from its undisturbed position.</p>","<p>For sound, a larger amplitude means a <strong>louder</strong> sound.</p>",0],["d-p12-frequency",12,"Frequency","<p>The number of waves passing a point (or produced by the source) per second.</p>","<p>Unit <strong>hertz (Hz)</strong>. For sound, a higher frequency means a <strong>higher pitch</strong>.</p>",0],["d-p12-echo",12,"Echo","<p>An echo is the reflection of sound waves.</p>","<p>Hard, flat surfaces reflect sound well.</p>",0],["d-p12-ultrasound",12,"Ultrasound","<p>Sound with a frequency higher than 20 kHz.</p>","<p>Too high for humans to hear.</p>",0],["d-p13-normal-angle-of-incidence-refl",13,"Normal, angle of incidence, reflection and refraction","<p>The normal is a line at 90° to a surface at the point where the ray hits it.</p><p>The angles of incidence, reflection and refraction are measured between the ray and the normal.</p>","",0],["d-p13-refraction",13,"Refraction","<p>The change in direction of light when it passes from one medium into another, because its speed changes.</p>","<p>Light <strong>slows down</strong> entering a denser medium (e.g. air → glass) and bends <strong>towards</strong> the normal; it speeds up leaving and bends <strong>away</strong> from the normal. A ray along the normal does not bend.</p>",0],["d-p13-refractive-index-n",13,"Refractive index, n<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The ratio of the speeds of a wave in two different regions.</p>","<p>n = speed of light in air (vacuum) ÷ speed of light in the material. It measures how much the material bends (refracts) light. Glass: n ≈ 1.5. No unit.</p>",1],["d-p13-internal-reflection",13,"Internal reflection","<p>When a ray of light strikes the inner surface of a material and some of the light reflects back inside it.</p>","",0],["d-p13-critical-angle",13,"Critical angle","<p>The angle of incidence inside the denser medium for which the angle of refraction is 90°.</p>","<p>If the angle of incidence is <strong>greater</strong> than the critical angle, total internal reflection happens. Glass: c ≈ 42°.</p>",0],["d-p13-total-internal-reflection-tir",13,"Total internal reflection (TIR)","<p>When light inside a denser medium hits the boundary at an angle of incidence greater than the critical angle, and all of the light is reflected back inside.</p>","<p>Examples: <strong>optical fibres</strong>, prisms in periscopes and binoculars, the sparkle of diamonds.</p>",0],["d-p13-dispersion",13,"Dispersion","<p>The separation of white light into colours (different wavelengths) because each colour is refracted through a different angle.</p>","<p>Shown when white light passes through a glass prism. Violet is refracted most, red least.</p>",0],["d-p13-monochromatic-light",13,"Monochromatic light<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Visible light of a single frequency is described as monochromatic.</p>","<p>e.g. light from a laser.</p>",1],["d-p13-principal-axis",13,"Principal axis","<p>The line passing through the centre of a lens, perpendicular to its surface.</p>","",0],["d-p13-principal-focus-focal-point",13,"Principal focus (focal point)","<p>The point at which rays of light parallel to the principal axis converge after passing through a converging lens.</p>","",0],["d-p13-focal-length",13,"Focal length","<p>The distance from the centre of the lens to its principal focus.</p>","<p>A fatter (more curved) lens has a shorter focal length.</p>",0],["d-p13-real-and-virtual-images",13,"Real and virtual images","<p>A real image can be formed on a screen; a virtual image cannot.</p><p>A virtual image is formed where diverging rays are extrapolated backwards.</p>","",0],["d-p14-wavelength",14,"Wavelength, λ","<p>The distance between two adjacent crests (or two adjacent troughs) of a wave.</p>","<p>Unit m (or cm, nm).</p>",0],["d-p14-crest-trough-and-amplitude",14,"Crest, trough and amplitude","<p>A crest (peak) is the highest point of a wave; a trough is the lowest point.</p><p>Amplitude is the greatest height or depth of a wave from its undisturbed position.</p>","",0],["d-p14-wave-speed",14,"Wave speed","<p>The distance travelled by a wave per unit time.</p>","<p>It is the speed at which the crests (and the energy) move. Unit m/s.</p>",0],["d-p14-wavefront",14,"Wavefront","<p>A line joining adjacent points on a wave that are all in step with each other.</p>","<p>e.g. the line along each crest in a ripple tank. The wavelength is the distance between neighbouring wavefronts.</p>",0],["d-p14-transverse-wave",14,"Transverse wave","<p>A wave in which the direction of vibration is at right angles to the direction of propagation.</p>","<p>Examples: <strong>electromagnetic radiation</strong>, <strong>water waves</strong>, seismic <strong>S-waves</strong> (secondary).</p>",0],["d-p14-longitudinal-wave",14,"Longitudinal wave","<p>A wave in which the direction of vibration is parallel to the direction of propagation.</p>","<p>Examples: <strong>sound waves</strong>, seismic <strong>P-waves</strong> (primary). They have compressions and rarefactions.</p>",0],["d-p14-diffraction",14,"Diffraction","<p>When a wave spreads out as it travels through a gap or past the edge of an object.</p>","<p>The wavelength and speed do not change.</p>",0],["d-p15-analogue-signal",15,"Analogue signal<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A signal that varies continuously in amplitude or frequency.</p>","",1],["d-p15-digital-signal",15,"Digital signal<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A signal that is a series of pulses with only two values, on and off (1 and 0).</p>","",1],["d-p16-magnetic-and-non-magnetic-mate",16,"Magnetic and non-magnetic materials","<p>Magnetic materials are attracted by a magnet and can be magnetised; non-magnetic materials are not.</p>","<p>Magnetic: <strong>iron, steel, nickel, cobalt</strong>. Non-magnetic: copper, aluminium, wood, plastic, glass.</p>",0],["d-p16-induced-magnetism",16,"Induced magnetism","<p>When a magnetic material is only magnetised while it is placed in a magnetic field.</p>","<p>e.g. an iron nail near the N pole of a magnet: the end nearest the N pole becomes an S pole, so the nail is attracted.</p>",0],["d-p16-magnetic-field",16,"Magnetic field","<p>A region in which a magnetic pole experiences a force.</p>","",0],["d-p16-direction-of-a-magnetic-field",16,"Direction of a magnetic field","<p>The direction of a magnetic field at a point is the direction of the force on the N pole of a magnet at that point.</p>","<p>So field lines go from <strong>N to S</strong> outside a magnet.</p>",0],["d-p17-electric-field",17,"Electric field<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A region in which an electric charge experiences a force.</p>","",1],["d-p17-direction-of-an-electric-field",17,"Direction of an electric field<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The direction of an electric field at a point is the direction of the force on a positive charge at that point.</p>","<p>So field lines point <strong>away from positive</strong> and <strong>towards negative</strong> charges.</p>",1],["d-p18-conductor",18,"Conductor","<p>A material that allows an electric current to flow through it.</p>","<p>Metals (e.g. copper) and graphite. Simple electron model: they contain <strong>free electrons</strong> that can move through the material.</p>",0],["d-p18-insulator",18,"Insulator","<p>A material that does not allow an electric current to flow through it.</p>","<p>e.g. plastic, rubber, glass, wood. The electrons are held tightly in their atoms and cannot move freely.</p>",0],["d-p18-electric-current",18,"Electric current<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Current is the charge passing a point per unit time.</p>","<p>I = Q ÷ t. Unit <strong>ampere (A)</strong>. Measured with an <strong>ammeter in series</strong>.</p>",1],["d-p18-conventional-current",18,"Conventional current<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Conventional current is from positive to negative; the flow of free electrons is from negative to positive.</p>","",1],["d-p18-alternating-current-a-c",18,"Alternating current (a.c.)","<p>Electric current that (periodically) changes direction.</p>","<p>e.g. mains electricity.</p>",0],["d-p18-direct-current-d-c",18,"Direct current (d.c.)","<p>Electric current that flows in the same direction all the time.</p>","<p>e.g. from a cell or battery.</p>",0],["d-p18-electromotive-force-e-m-f",18,"Electromotive force (e.m.f.)","<p>The electrical work done by a source in moving a unit charge around a complete circuit.</p>","<p>Measured in <strong>volts (V)</strong>. It is the energy the source gives to each coulomb.</p>",0],["d-p18-potential-difference-p-d",18,"Potential difference (p.d.)","<p>The work done by a unit charge passing through a component.</p>","<p>Measured in <strong>volts (V)</strong> with a <strong>voltmeter in parallel</strong>. It is the energy each coulomb transfers to the component.</p>",0],["d-p18-resistance",18,"Resistance","<p>Resistance = potential difference ÷ current.</p>","<p>R = V ÷ I. Unit <strong>ohm (Ω)</strong>. It tells you how hard it is for current to flow through a component.</p>",0],["d-p18-diode",18,"Diode<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A component that allows current to flow in one direction only.</p>","<p>Very high resistance in the reverse direction.</p>",1],["d-p18-light-emitting-diode-led",18,"Light-emitting diode (LED)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A diode that gives out light when current flows through it in the forward direction.</p>","<p>Uses little power; used as indicator lights.</p>",1],["d-p18-kilowatt-hour-kw-h",18,"Kilowatt-hour (kW h)","<p>The energy transferred by a 1 kW appliance in 1 hour.</p>","<p>1 kW h = 3 600 000 J (3.6 MJ). <strong>Cost = number of kW h × price per kW h.</strong></p>",0],["d-p19-light-dependent-resistor-ldr",19,"Light-dependent resistor (LDR)","<p>A resistor whose resistance decreases when the light intensity on it increases.</p>","<p>Used in light-sensing circuits (e.g. street lights that switch on at night).</p>",0],["d-p19-thermistor-ntc",19,"Thermistor (NTC)","<p>A resistor whose resistance decreases as its temperature increases.</p>","<p>Used in temperature-sensing circuits (e.g. fire alarms, thermostats). Only NTC thermistors are in the syllabus.</p>",0],["d-p19-relay",19,"Relay","<p>A switch operated by an electromagnet.</p>","<p>A <strong>small current</strong> in the coil switches on a separate circuit carrying a <strong>large current</strong> (e.g. a car starter motor), keeping the user safe.</p>",0],["d-p19-variable-resistor",19,"Variable resistor","<p>A resistor whose resistance can be changed.</p>","<p>Used to control the current, e.g. dimmer switches and volume controls.</p>",0],["d-p19-potential-divider",19,"Potential divider<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Part of a circuit consisting of two resistors connected in series to obtain a smaller voltage than supplied.</p>","<p>The supply p.d. is shared in the ratio of the resistances: <strong>the bigger resistor gets the bigger share</strong>. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Variable potential divider:</strong> if one resistor is an LDR or thermistor, its share of the p.d. changes with light or temperature, so the output voltage can switch on a lamp or alarm.</p>",1],["d-p19-fuse",19,"Fuse","<p>A thin piece of wire that melts and breaks the circuit when the current exceeds its rating.</p>","<p>It protects the cables and appliance from overheating. <strong>Choose a rating just above the normal working current</strong> (e.g. 3 A, 5 A or 13 A). Fitted in the <strong>live</strong> wire.</p>",0],["d-p19-trip-switch-circuit-breaker",19,"Trip switch (circuit breaker)","<p>A safety switch that opens automatically (trips) when the current exceeds a set value.</p>","<p>It can be <strong>reset</strong> instead of replaced, and acts faster than a fuse. Choose a setting just above the normal current.</p>",0],["d-p19-earthing",19,"Earthing","<p>Connecting the metal case of an appliance to the earth wire.</p>","<p>If the live wire touches the case, a <strong>large current</strong> flows through the earth wire, which <strong>melts the fuse</strong> (or trips the switch) and cuts off the supply, so the case does not stay live.</p>",0],["d-p19-double-insulation",19,"Double insulation","<p>An appliance whose outer casing is made of a non-conducting material (plastic), so it cannot become live.</p>","<p>It needs <strong>no earth wire</strong>; a fuse still protects the circuit and the cabling.</p>",0],["d-p20-motor-effect",20,"Motor effect","<p>When current flows in a wire in a magnetic field which is not parallel to the current, a force is exerted on the wire.</p>","<p>Reversing <strong>either</strong> the current <strong>or</strong> the field reverses the force. No force acts if the wire is parallel to the field.</p>",0],["d-p21-electromagnetic-induction",21,"Electromagnetic induction","<p>An e.m.f. is induced in a conductor when it moves across a magnetic field, or when a changing magnetic field links with the conductor.</p>","<p>If the conductor is part of a complete circuit, an <strong>induced current</strong> flows.</p>",0],["d-p21-transformer",21,"Transformer","<p>A device that changes the voltage of an alternating supply, made of a primary coil and a secondary coil wound on a soft-iron core.</p>","<p><strong>Step-up:</strong> more turns on the secondary, so the output voltage is higher. <strong>Step-down:</strong> fewer turns on the secondary, so the output voltage is lower.</p>",0],["d-p22-proton-number-atomic-number-z",22,"Proton number (atomic number), Z","<p>The number of protons in the nucleus.</p>","<p>It decides which element the atom is.</p>",0],["d-p22-nucleon-number-mass-number-a",22,"Nucleon number (mass number), A","<p>The number of protons plus neutrons (nucleons) in the nucleus.</p>","",0],["d-p22-isotopes",22,"Isotopes","<p>Atoms of the same element with the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.</p>","<p>(Same Z, different A.) An element may have more than one isotope, e.g. carbon-12 and carbon-14.</p>",0],["d-p23-background-radiation",23,"Background radiation","<p>The radiation from the environment to which we are exposed all the time.</p>","<p>Main sources: <strong>radon gas</strong> (in the air), <strong>rocks and buildings</strong>, <strong>food and drink</strong>, and <strong>cosmic rays</strong>.</p>",0],["d-p23-count-rate",23,"Count rate<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The number of decays recorded per second or per minute by a detector connected to a counter.</p>","<p>Unit counts/s or counts/minute. Ionising radiation is measured with a detector (e.g. a Geiger–Müller tube) connected to a counter. <span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>Corrected count rate = measured count rate − background count rate.</strong></p>",1],["d-p23-radioactivity",23,"Radioactivity","<p>The spontaneous and random emission of radiation (α, β or γ) from an unstable nucleus.</p>","<p>The emission is random in direction, and you cannot predict when a particular nucleus will decay.</p>",0],["d-p23-radioactive-decay",23,"Radioactive decay","<p>A change in an unstable nucleus that can result in the emission of α-particles or β-particles and/or γ-radiation.</p>","<p>The changes are <strong>spontaneous and random</strong>. During α-decay or β-decay, the nucleus changes to that of a <strong>different element</strong>.</p>",0],["d-p23-radioisotopes",23,"Radioisotopes<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Isotopes that are radioactive because their nuclei are unstable.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> A nucleus may be unstable because it has an <strong>excess of neutrons</strong> and/or because it is <strong>too heavy</strong>.</p>",1],["d-p23-alpha-decay",23,"Alpha decay<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The decay of an unstable nucleus by emitting an α-particle (2 protons + 2 neutrons).</p>","<p>The <strong>nucleon number falls by 4</strong> and the <strong>proton number falls by 2</strong>. e.g. <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>226</span><span>88</span></span>Ra</span> → <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>222</span><span>86</span></span>Rn</span> + <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>4</span><span>2</span></span>α</span>.</p>",1],["d-p23-beta-decay",23,"Beta decay<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The decay of an unstable nucleus by emitting a β-particle (a fast electron).</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> In the nucleus, <strong>neutron → proton + electron</strong>, so the <strong>proton number rises by 1</strong> and the <strong>nucleon number stays the same</strong>; the number of excess neutrons is reduced. e.g. <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>14</span><span>6</span></span>C</span> → <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>14</span><span>7</span></span>N</span> + <span class=\"nuc\"><span class=\"ns\"><span>0</span><span>−1</span></span>β</span>.</p>",1],["d-p23-ionisation",23,"Ionisation","<p>When an atom or molecule gains or loses electrons and becomes charged.</p>","",0],["d-p23-half-life",23,"Half-life","<p>The half-life of a particular isotope is the time taken for half the nuclei of that isotope in any sample to decay.</p>","<p>After each half-life, the count rate (and the number of undecayed nuclei) halves. Half-lives range from fractions of a second to billions of years.</p>",0],["d-p24-accretion-disc",24,"Accretion disc","<p>A rotating disc of gas and dust around a forming star, from which the planets formed.</p>","",0],["d-p24-elliptical-orbits",24,"Elliptical orbits<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Planets, minor planets and comets have elliptical orbits; the Sun is not at the centre of the ellipse, except when the orbit is approximately circular.</p>","<p>An ellipse is a stretched circle. Comets have very elongated orbits.</p>",1],["d-p24-average-orbital-speed",24,"Average orbital speed<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Average orbital speed v = 2πr ÷ T, where r is the average radius of the orbit and T is the orbital period.</p>","<p>It is the distance round the orbit divided by the time for one orbit.</p>",1],["d-p25-light-year",25,"Light-year<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The distance travelled in (the vacuum of) space by light in one year.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> <strong>1 light-year = 9.5 × 10¹⁵ m.</strong> It is a unit of <strong>distance</strong>, not time.</p>",1],["d-p25-protostar",25,"Protostar<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>An interstellar cloud collapsing and increasing in temperature as a result of its internal gravitational attraction.</p>","<p>Stars form from <strong>interstellar clouds of gas and dust</strong> that contain hydrogen.</p>",1],["d-p25-stable-star",25,"Stable star<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>A protostar becomes a stable star when the inward force of gravitational attraction is balanced by an outward force due to the high temperature in the centre of the star.</p>","",1],["d-p25-supernova",25,"Supernova<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The explosion of a red supergiant, forming a nebula containing hydrogen and new heavier elements, and leaving behind a neutron star or a black hole at its centre.</p>","<p>The nebula may later form <strong>new stars with orbiting planets</strong>.</p>",1],["d-p25-redshift",25,"Redshift","<p>An increase in the observed wavelength of electromagnetic radiation emitted from receding stars and galaxies.</p>","<p>The light from distant galaxies appears <strong>redshifted</strong> compared with light emitted on Earth: the spectral lines move towards the <strong>red</strong> (longer wavelength) end. (It is like the drop in pitch of an ambulance siren moving away, the Doppler effect.)</p>",0],["d-p25-cosmic-microwave-background-ra",25,"Cosmic microwave background radiation (CMBR)<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>Microwave radiation of a specific frequency observed at all points in space around us.</p>","<p><span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span> It was produced <strong>shortly after the Universe was formed</strong> and has been <strong>stretched (expanded) into the microwave region</strong> as the Universe expanded. It is further evidence for the Big Bang.</p>",1],["d-p25-hubble-constant-h",25,"Hubble constant, H₀<span class=\"qn-ext\">EXT</span>","<p>The ratio of the speed at which a galaxy is moving away from the Earth to its distance from the Earth.</p>","<p>H₀ = v ÷ d. Unit <strong>s⁻¹</strong> (per second).</p>",1]]});
